
Retrieve:
























Badge #38 -- Ash Water & Clean Hands

Mark: ash scoop · hanging bag · open hand
Vow: Keep sickness out before it learns our names.
	1.	White ash
After cookfire, Hakaka raked soft white ash to one side. "Not the black. White only," she said. La-la saved it in a dry pot with a lid.
	2.	Hang the bag
Taro wove a reed cone and lined it with a clean scrap. They tied it to a branch over a bowl. Ya-la pressed a small plug in the tip. "No leaks yet."
	3.	First pour
Sa-la brought water just off boil. "Slow," Hakaka said. He poured. The ash drank, then wept a pale tea into the bowl when Ya-la eased the plug. It smelled sharp.
	4.	Test
La-la dipped a reed, rubbed two fingers. "Slick," she said, then wiped it off fast. Hakaka held a feather under a drop. It curled and thinned. "Strong enough. Not for mouth. Not for eyes."
	5.	Small soap
They melted a thumb of fat in a clay cup near coals. Hakaka fed ash-water by drops and stirred with a stick until the mix pulled like thread. She poured it into a shell. It cooled to a soft cake. "Slip," Ya-la named it.
	6.	Work order
"Here," Hakaka said, holding the cake. "Latrine--hands to wrists. Meat--before and after. Cough--after cloth. Cistern--before you touch the lid." La-la: "Two breaths per rub. Slow ones."
	7.	First use
Taro’s palm held a small cut from reed. He hissed when ash-water found it, then grinned. "Bites, then forgives." Ya-la rinsed with clean, held his hands to air. "Dry before touch."
	8.	Cistern guard
Before lifting the lid, Sa-la scrubbed with slip at the cloth hole, then rinsed with a ladle. La-la tapped his wrist. "Hands only. Ladle ties stay tied."
	9.	Store and mark
They set the ash pot high, the slip shell on a flat stone by the cistern. Hakaka scratched a hand on the lid’s rim. "This hand first," she said.
	10.	Child rule
The old woman watched, eyes narrow. "No small fingers near ash," she said. Hakaka nodded. "If you carry, you carry high."
	11.	Soaproot trade
Across the river the scarred woman crushed a thick leaf in her palms; it foamed. She held it up, then pointed at her hair. Ya-la held the slip cake, pointed to hands, then to the lid cloth. They sent a piece each across. The boy laughed at the bubbles.
	12.	Wash line
Evening. They set a reed line low. From left to right: latrine scrap, meat scrap, cough scrap. "Clean left to right," La-la said. Sa-la reached wrong once; Taro thumped his shoulder. "Order keeps you fed."
	13.	Burn and bury
Spent cloths went to coals or a deep hole. "Not back to the wind," Hakaka said.
	14.	Cough note
A week. The old woman cleared her throat and drank. "Less rasp," she said. No one answered. They just held their bowls a little steadier.
	15.	Patch and keep
When the slip thinned in its shell, Hakaka scraped the last soft into a new mix and stirred until it shined. "Waste is a door we don’t open," Ya-la said.

Ash ledger mark: a cone dripping into a bowl · a shell beside a hand
-- White ash only; black sleeps.
-- Drip slow; test with feather.
-- Slip for hands, not mouths.
-- Two slow breaths per wash.
-- Clean before cistern, meat, and cough.
-- Cloths burn or bury.
-- Little ones high from ash.
-- Trade clean both ways.

Badge #37 -- Rain Teeth & Quiet Cistern

Mark: broken-line roof · clay jar with lid
Vow: Catch clean; leave dirt where it wants to settle.

1) Find the fall
Hakaka watched the eaves in dry light, then spit on the ridge. The spit walked to the low edge. "Here," she said. La-la drove two forked sticks under the drip line.

2) Teeth
Sa-la split reeds and cut them into short, notched pieces. "Teeth," Ya-la said. They lashed the reeds along a long rib, mouths open. When he shook it, the water paths showed.

3) First hang
They tied the teeth-rib under the eave with bark. Taro wedged a tilt so the end pointed toward a shallow pit. "Fall wants slope," he said. The old woman grunted: "So do fools."

4) Test
Ya-la poured a gourd at the ridge. Beads ran, gathered, slid along the teeth, and fell in a narrow ribbon. The ribbon hit the pit’s lip and splashed wide. La-la set a flat stone there. The splash obeyed.

5) Cistern mouth
They scraped a bowl in the earth a hand deep with a smaller bowl below it, off to one side. Hakaka lined both with clay, thumb-smooth. "Big for holding, little for dirt."

6) Coat & fire
Sa-la warmed the clay with coals until it turned dull and hard. Not hot enough to crack, just enough to set. The pit smelled like wet stone. The old woman’s eyes eased.

7) First rinse
La-la let the first run of roof water go to the little bowl. Dead leaf and grit settled there. When the water cleared, Ya-la pinched the reed plug and let clean spill into the big bowl.

8) Lid and cloth
Taro wove a tight reed circle. Ya-la pressed clay over it to make a lid with a breathing hole. "Cloth," Hakaka said. They tied a clean scrap under the hole. "Drink from above only."

9) Ladle law
Sa-la reached with his hand. La-la tapped his knuckles with a stick. "Ladle." He carved a shallow spoon from split gourd and tied it to the lid with cord so it would not wander.

10) Mosquito mind
At dusk Ya-la laid damp mint leaves under the cloth. The air smelled sharp. No singing at their ears when they slept.

11) Silt duty
Each morning Taro lifted the plug on the small bowl and let yesterday’s dirt slide back to the ground away from the mouth. He packed a fresh pinch of ash on the rim. "Bite-stopper," he said.

12) Taste
They drank. Cool, a little like clay, not like river. The old woman said, "My cough sits down."

13) Patch
A seam at the big bowl wept. Hakaka pressed thin clay into the line and rubbed until it shined. "Wet finds weak. We find it first."

14) Share line
Across the river, the scarred woman watched the teeth glitter. Ya-la lifted the ladle and pointed to the cloth, then to the small bowl. The woman nodded and drew a square in the air--lid.

15) Law & mark
On the ash ledger Hakaka drew a roof with teeth and two bowls, small feeding big. Under it she marked:
-- First run to dirt bowl.
-- Cloth at the breath hole.
-- Ladle tied, hands out.
-- Mint nights.
-- Ash on lip.
-- Patch thin, rub long.

The cistern held quiet. The roof spoke less nonsense when it rained. They drank and did not pay with fever.

Badge #36 -- Wind Door, Shade Wall, and Sleep

Mark: doorway with a bent ear · three shade lines
Vow: Let air work; don’t make it angry.

1) Face
Hakaka stood in the door gap and licked a finger. Wind came from the scrub, not the river. "Turn it." La-la shifted the doorway two hands toward the leeward side. The old woman nodded once. "Less bite."

2) Ear test
Ya-la put her ear to the gap. It hissed at the sharp edge. She rubbed the post with wet clay and smoothed it with a leaf. The hiss went down to a soft talk. "Door likes round," she said.

3) High mouth, low mouth
Taro cut a small eye up high near the smoke place. Sa-la opened a hand-wide slot low, by the floor. "Hot out the high, cool in the low," Hakaka said. They watched the ash drift leave by the eye. Good.

4) Shade wall
They set a reed mat two steps outside, offset from the door. Bark strips on the top edge, pegged tight. La-la checked the shadow at noon. "Covers the mouth when the sun is cruel."

5) Fly curtain
Ya-la twisted grass into a bead chain and hung it inside the door. Each bead held a drop of mint water. Flies tasted and left. The old woman smiled with the side of her mouth that still liked jokes.

6) Threshold
Sa-la leveled a lip of hard clay at the door line. "Keeps sweep work in." He pressed a thread of ash into it with his thumb. "So feet remember to lift."

7) Night draft
They laid down. Heat rose and walked out the high eye. Cool slid in at their ankles. The robe moved once, slow and kind, but did not tug. Nobody woke angry.

8) Morning check
No soot on the sleeping cloth. No dust dune inside the door. The mint beads were dry and quiet. The old woman said, "I didn’t cough like a dog." Ya-la refreshed the beads and wiped the clay lip smooth.

9) Law & mark
On the ash ledger Hakaka drew a door with a bent ear and two small mouths, one high, one low. Under it she marked:
-- Door leeward.
-- Edges round.
-- High lets go, low invites.
-- Shade outside, beads inside.
-- Lift at the lip.

The shelter breathed even when they were still. The day started softer.

Badge #35 -- Cool Pit, Shelf, and Keep

Mark: square pit with cross-hatch lid · one fly marked with an X
Vow: What we save should still be food.

1) Choosing
Taro tapped ground near the shade wall. "Here. Morning cool. No flood." Hakaka checked with her heel; the dirt was packed and dry. Ya-la watched the ant line. "They don’t live under." Good.

2) Dig
They cut a square the length of La-la’s arm, the depth of Sa-la’s elbow. Sa-la shoveled with a rib scraper; the old woman flicked stones out with a stick. The hole held its sides. "Enough," Hakaka said. "We are not burying a house."

3) Line
Clay milk on the wall, thin. Ash sifted over it, rubbed in. La-la set a flat stone on the bottom so jars wouldn’t drink the dirt. Ya-la ran her fingers along the corners. "No crumbs." She blew once and the loose dust left.

4) Lid
Reed mat, tight weave. Bark frame. Taro fixed a cross-brace so it wouldn’t sag. Sa-la tied a small loop to pull it easy. The old woman tapped the lid. "Mice argue with gaps." La-la added one more strip. The tap came back dull. "Good."

5) First keep
They cooled the new jar with a wet cloth and set it in. Dried meat sat in a clay bowl, covered. Roots in a net bag, washed and dry. Ya-la laid a mint leaf on top. "Nose will tell." Hakaka lowered the lid and pegged it down.

6) Fly lesson
By noon a smell climbed. Not bad, but trying. Sa-la lifted the lid. Two flies slipped in and loved everything. Hakaka’s hand closed the lid on his fingers. "Out," she said. He hissed and backed away. Ya-la smoked the pit with a twist of dung, lid half-open, then closed it and let the smoke settle. The smell softened. "Flies pay rent with smoke," the old woman said.

7) Check
Evening. They opened and tasted. Meat was still dry and honest. Roots were cool, not sweet with rot. The jar’s neck wet but not sweating hard. La-la tied a small bell of pebbles to the lid. "Hands remember, but bells are louder."

8) Law & mark
On the ash ledger Hakaka drew the pit with the reed lid and crossed one fly. Under it she marked:
-- Thin clay, thin ash.
-- Lid tight. Smoke if flies.
-- First taste is small.
-- Close what you open.

They pegged the lid again. The pit kept quiet. The night carried heat past their shelter. What they saved waited like it meant to last.

Badge #34 -- Jar, Fire, and Carry

Mark: jar with two ears scratched in ash · three dots above it (full, half, empty)
Vow: Water walks with us.

1) Clay bed
Ya-la found the heavy earth under the bank, cut it in squares. La-la broke dry sand with a stick and folded it in. "Grit makes it listen," she said. Sa-la crushed old shell between stones until it turned to a soft bite. They mixed until it stopped grabbing at their hands.

2) Coil
Hakaka rolled ropes in her palm, set a circle, and climbed it slow. Ya-la slipped thin clay at each join and pressed with the heel of her thumb. Sa-la hurried; a seam opened. Hakaka lifted his hands and set them down on the table of her own. "Small and steady." He breathed and the wall learned his pace.

3) Ears
Taro pinched two lugs high on the shoulder. "Ears carry," he said. La-la smoothed the lip so a cloth could sit clean. The old woman tapped the rim with a knuckle until the sound ran clear and round. "No cracks arguing," she said, and spat once for luck.

4) Dry
They set the jar on warm ash and turned it when the skin took dull. Sa-la wanted sun; Hakaka shaded it with a flat of bark. "Fast breaks," she said. He nodded and sat on his hands. By dusk the seam-lines slept.

5) Fire
A small ring of shards, dry twigs, then dung. Ya-la fed it with patience. Smoke wrapped the jar, then heat. When the jar began to talk too high, Hakaka narrowed the draw hole with a flat stone. One small bowl beside it cracked on the shoulder and fell in two. No one cursed. La-la only said, "Keep another day ready to break," and fed more of the slow fire.

6) Test
Morning: pale, quiet jar. They warmed it with breath, then poured a bowl of river in. A thin sweat showed at one seam and then sealed as the wall drank and swelled. Ya-la rubbed clay-milk over the ears and let it set. Taro tied a wet strip around the lip to test the pull. It held.

7) Strap
Bark cord, doubled. Grass pad where it would cut. Ya-la showed the back-carry. Hakaka lifted the filled jar to La-la’s shoulders; La-la stood into it. "Heavy," she said. "Honest." Sa-la tried, staggered two steps, caught himself, and grinned. The old woman drank first from a leaf cup, two sips only. "Cold. Good."

8) Law & mark
On the ash ledger Hakaka drew the jar with ears, three dots above it. Underneath she marked four short lines:
-- Grit listens.
-- Fast breaks.
-- First mouth is shared.
-- High and shade.

They set the jar in the cool corner, tied it to a peg so it wouldn’t wander in sleep. Outside, the river went on saying what it always says. Inside, water waited without spilling.

Badge #33 -- Split, Smoke, and Share

Mark: forked rack sketched in ash · three fish icons, one turned back
Vow: Feed today, remember tomorrow.

1) Pick & Split
They chose two thick fish from the basket--good oil, tight flesh. La-la set the knife just off the spine and pulled clean. Taro held the tail and kept his fingers. Sa-la reached for a belly strip. Hakaka tapped his wrist. "Taste waits." He put it back; the want stayed in his mouth.

2) Rack
They drove two forked sticks, laid a green pole, then crossed slender rods like a loose ladder. Ya-la rubbed a little clay where wood met wood. "Slow fire likes tight." The old woman squinted. "And wind hates corners." La-la shifted the rack to leeward a hand’s width. The rack settled and agreed.

3) Smoke
Wet chips, cool fire. Sa-la breathed until the ember colored, then fed bark no bigger than a finger. Smoke came pale and steady, a soft coat. Taro hung the splits skin-down, space between. Hakaka checked the lift of each tongue of smoke and covered the draw hole with a flat stone until the fire stopped talking fast.

4) Dogs
Dust lifted where the thin dog liked to try. La-la set a thorn branch under the rack and tied a bone rattle to it low. "If it learns, we learn too," she said. Sa-la took a step toward the strip that fell by itself. Hakaka shook her head once. "Dog’s lesson." He nodded, eyes down, and stood still. The dog decided it loved thorns more than fish and went elsewhere.

5) Across
Ya-la wrapped one small split in wet leaves, tied it tidy, and put two pebbles in with it--again + with. She sent the pouch on the reed line. The boy caught it now without thinking. The scar woman smelled, smiled in the eyes only, and set their own packet riding back: salt rubbed in cloth, a pinch only, sharp as a clean laugh. The cliff man lifted a palm once, then went to cut more stakes.

6) First taste
At last light the surface took color. Hakaka pinched a corner, cooled it from one breath, broke it in four. Old woman first. "About time." La-la chewed once and let the oil sit in her mouth. Sa-la swallowed too fast, coughed, then grinned without asking for more. Ya-la put a grain of the gift salt on her thumb and touched each piece. "Not always," she said. "Enough today."

7) Store
They threaded the rest on a reed cord and hung it high on the cold side of rock. Taro carved a notch in the fork for a future cord that wasn’t there yet. "I’ll like tomorrow," he said. La-la wiped the knife, dried it on her sleeve, then gave it to Sa-la to wrap and tie to the robe. "If it falls," she said, "we learn again."

8) Law & mark
On the ash ledger Hakaka drew: a ladder for rack, a soft cloud for smoke, a dot-star for salt, and a small circle above the line for food kept high. She pressed the end of a split into ash and left a faint fish-print that did not last. Then she swept it away and spoke the part that did: "First piece rides. Fallen piece feeds dogs. Taste waits. High keeps."

They slept with the room smelling like quiet rain and warm wood. Outside, the rack held its shape, and the smoke learned the path on its own.

Badge #32 -- Weir Night Watch

Mark: three notches cut on near stake · chewed scrap tied back
Vow: Watch with eyes, not hunger.

1) Sign

Dawn showed small damage. One wattle loosened, law ribbon chewed. Mud held a print like a thumb with whiskers. Taro squatted, touched the dent. "Otter," he said. La-la checked the lattice with a flat palm. "Took one. Left the rest." Across, the scar woman lifted the same chewed blue. Her mouth said: saw. Hakaka lifted her hand: yes.

2) Set the watch

"We’ll sit," Hakaka said. "Two and two." She tapped the stake: first notch, second, third--turns. Sa-la’s chin rose. "I’ll take first." La-la tied the bone rattle to his wrist with a short cord. "If it comes, shake river, not rocks," she said. Ya-la slipped a coal-warm scrap into his palm. "For your fingers, not for the weir." Across, the boy held up his own rattle--a string of shells. The cliff man set two fingers to his eyes, then pointed downriver: I’ll take below.

3) Night

Cold slid long. The water talked small. Sa-la counted breaths to stay awake, then lost count and started over. A slick shape stitched the surface and vanished. He lifted the rattle and gave it one hard shake. Sound ran the bars, thin and sharp. Across, shells answered--two quick, one long. The slick head rose once more, decided it hated music, and went dark.

Later, when the cold crept under his knees, sleep leaned on him. He blinked, heavy, and woke to a tiny scrape. The ribbon twitched. He shook again, too late for one silver body already slipped. He felt his jaw go tight. He kept the rattle moving until his hand burned with it.

4) Account

Morning, he told it straight. "I shook. Lost one." La-la checked his eyes and nodded once. "Good. Keep the numbers honest." The old woman spat into the water, soft. "Pay with better next." Across, the woman held up two small willow sticks cut to points and mimed a throat. Hakaka smiled in her eyes. "Throat fence," she said. "Let’s teach each other."

5) Throat fence

They set short, slanted twigs just inside the mouth where a thief would try to slide through soft. Taro tapped each point with a pebble until it hummed in the wood. Ya-la sent a pouch with three: one to place, one to copy, one to keep. The boy returned theirs with the same, plus a sliver of smooth bone--grease-polished--good for easing points through tight weave. La-la used it and grunted approval.

6) Trial run

They ran the weir at last light. Two fish only, fat enough for stew. A small one faced the sticks, thought, and turned back upstream. Ya-la touched the law ribbon and breathed out. Across, the woman saw the turn and pressed her palm to the stake and left it there a beat.

Dogs came to the bank again, thinking old thoughts. Two rattles spoke at once--bone and shell. The dogs made new choices and left.

7) Law

Hakaka cut the third notch for the watch kept. "Three nights, then rest," she said. "If you lose one, you say it. If you save one, you don’t sing it." Sa-la’s ears went a little red; he nodded.

On the ash ledger she marked: a small crown for otter, a comb of short lines for the throat fence, a turned-fish, and three tiny cuts for watches done. Then she swept it flat. What mattered stayed in wrists, in how the sticks leaned, in the way two rattles agreed across water.

They went home with enough and a rattle each still tied on.

Badge #31 -- Shared Weir

Mark: split-prong carved in stake · two pebbles at each root
Vow: Take enough. Leave mothers and first water.

1) Measure

Morning was low and clear. Taro walked the shallows with a stick, counting steps where the river thinned its voice. "Here," he said. La-la drove a test stake, heel and palm. The old woman called from the bank, "Teeth low there." Hakaka nodded. "Good. We want teeth, not a mouth."

Across, the scarred woman tapped once--ready. Ya-la answered once and pointed at the straight run between two willow shadows. The boy held up two fingers--two spans. Hakaka held up three--safer. The cliff man lifted the line: stop; then set his palm flat: slow.

2) Frame

They fed two stakes from both banks, angled into the current like careful knees. Tug code ran between wrists: one-short for press, two-short for ease, long-for-help. Sa-la leaned too soon; the stake bucked and sang in his hand. "Hands first," La-la said, not looking at him. He set his jaw and stayed with the push till the wobble quieted.

Across, the boy’s stake slipped. The woman caught it with a twist of her hips and a low grunt, then tapped three--hold--so the river could forget the mistake. They all waited together and pretended not to breathe. When the pull went soft, they finished the set.

3) Lattice

Wattle, then cross-piece. Taro threaded reeds through the stakes, thumbs black with wet. Ya-la sent a short pouch--spit of twine, two good knots--just to say with. The woman tied the same weave across; her hands were quick and plain. The cliff man set a heavy cross bar on their side; La-la matched it with a lighter piece and a rock. "Let it choose the point," Hakaka said. The water did, and the lattice bowed just enough.

4) First run

They tapped two-one--send a lot--and pulled a guiding line across the mouth. Silver flicked once, twice, then vanished. "Early," the old woman said. "Wait until the wait turns sweet." They waited. The river stacked small shadows against the bars. La-la lifted the basket. Three fish, not full-grown. She lifted the smallest, looked at Hakaka; Hakaka shook her head. Back it went, quick as mercy. Across, the boy saw and nodded like he had learned a secret.

They sent two fish over: one for proof, one for promise. The woman set a tiny pebble in the returning pouch--later--so both sides could believe in tomorrow.

5) Trouble

Dogs slid out of scrub on their bank, ribs like bows. Sa-la’s shoulders went hot. He reached for a throwing stone; Hakaka pressed his wrist down. "Rattle," she said. La-la shook the bone rattle sharp and flat; across, the boy shook his own. The dogs flinched at the doubled sound and thought better thoughts. One strip of dropped gut lay in the mud. No one picked it up. "Their lesson," the old woman said.

6) Law

Ya-la braided a thin reed and tied it to the near stake. "Law ribbon," she said. "Mothers pass." The scarred woman saw, glanced upriver, and tied a scrap of blue to her side’s stake. The cliff man put a hand on the cross bar and pressed, testing. It held.

They ran the weir a last time at dusk--just enough for soup and a thin fry. Hakaka touched the wet wood. "Weir lives if both do." She tapped two-slow and paused. Two-slow came back. Still there.

On the ash ledger she drew: two stakes, one line, a small fish turned back, then swept it with her palm. Knowledge stayed where it needed to--hands, wrists, the angle of a push. The river went on talking. They listened and went home with enough.

Badge #30 -- Line Talk

Mark: three knots at our root · two at theirs · tug code carved in bark
Vow: Say less. Say clear.

Morning, the bridge hummed low. Wind ran with the current and pushed small chop. Hakaka set the coil tidy and pointed to the willow root. "Messages," she said. "Not mouths."

La-la made a short stick with cuts:
-- one tap: ready
-- two: send
-- three: hold
-- long pull: help

She tied it beside the root with a bit of thong. "Look before you tug," she told Sa-la. He nodded and kept his hands behind his back.

Across, the scarred woman saw the stick, showed it to the boy and the cliff man. The boy counted the cuts with his finger, then tapped them into his own palm to learn the feel.

Trial. Ya-la set a pouch with nothing in it--just a loop to carry the talk. She tapped once. Across, the boy tapped once back, too fast and proud. Ya-la tapped twice. La-la fed the pouch. It slid and arrived. The woman did nothing--only tapped twice in return. Ya-la pulled it home empty. "Good," she said. "We can send nothing and still understand."

They tried speed next. Slow taps, then quick taps. The river stole the quick ones, made them into one blur. "Slow," Hakaka said. "Water eats hurry."

They agreed on a call sign: two slow taps, then a pause that you could count. The cliff man added a change of grip--line lifted high for stop. Everyone practiced until the rope spoke the same way in both wrists.

Midday, the current leaned hard. The boy across, eager, gave a long pull by mistake. La-la’s fingers tightened. "Help," Sa-la breathed, already reaching. Hakaka caught his wrist. Wait. Across, the woman put her hand over the boy’s, lifted the line high--stop--and then tapped three, careful. Hold. She opened her hand so they could see it was mistake, not trouble. Hakaka answered with the same three, then one--ready--to start the lesson again. The boy bowed his head. The cliff man’s mouth moved once, maybe a smile, maybe not.

They made words out of small strings:
-- one, two: "send a little"
-- two, one: "send a lot"
-- one, one, one: "are you there?"
-- flat-hand-lift, then two: "wind bad, wait"

Ya-la sang the patterns under her breath till they stuck. Sa-la tapped them on his thigh. The old woman shut her eyes and named them out loud without seeing the line. "Ready, send, hold, stop," she said, and coughed less when the smoke stayed out.

Message worth the day: salt low. Ya-la tapped one-two--send a little. Across, the woman tapped two, then fed a pouch with a thumb of salt sewn into skin. It came clean. La-la weighed it in her palm. "Enough," she said. They sent back dried fish, two fingers’ worth, with a thanks tapped into the rope instead of the air.

Near dusk, a small dark ran the far bank--dog or lean fox--and the boy reached to warn them with a long pull. The woman stopped him, then gave three taps--hold--and laid a pebble on top of their root, then another pebble to mean "seen, not ours." Ya-la grinned for no reason and set two pebbles the same way. Not every sight is a message.

They ended the day with a last exchange: nothing in the pouch, just two taps, a pause, and two taps back--the sound of still-there. Hakaka marked the ash ledger with four short lines and swept it clean.

Night came. The line stayed quiet and alive between the banks. No one spoke across. The river talked enough.

Badge #29 -- Quiet Bridge

Mark: grey scrap high · twin loops on both banks · flat palm for stop
Vow: The line works; bodies wait.

By the narrows the river ran cleaner and meaner. Pouches had been slipping off the soft seam and coming back with teeth marks of water. Hakaka pointed at two willow roots, one on each bank. "There," she said. "There."

La-la braided a leader: reed over reed, two small loops a hand apart. Sa-la held the coil and kept his feet still on purpose. Ya-la set three pebbles in a row--what goes first, then second, then last--and nodded to the far bank.

Across, the woman with the scar raised both hands empty. The cliff man stepped to his root and planted his weight. The boy bounced, then remembered, and folded his arms tight.

First send: a pouch with the leader and a little ring of bark to show where to bite. Sa-la threw flat. The current tried to teach another lesson; the leader ignored it and kissed the quiet edge. The woman knelt, threaded their line through the bark ring, and tied a square knot that would not lie.

Hands talk:
	•	fists up: tight
	•	open palms: ease
	•	two fingers walking: pull together
	•	flat palm still: stop

They pulled together. The river said no twice, then yes. The line lifted out of slap and found its hum. La-la wrapped her end on the willow root and drove in a forked peg. Across, the cliff man did the same. The hum settled low in the wrist.

Test run: Hakaka loaded a pouch with a small stone and a strip of fat. Ya-la tapped twice on the line--go. The pouch slid like a bead on a string, rode the center, and arrived without learning to swim. The woman set in a twist of cured skin--thin, strong--then tapped twice back. The pouch came home the same way it left.

Sa-la’s toes crept to the edge. "I could--"

"No," Hakaka said, not loud. He stepped back and swallowed the rest.

They set signs beside the roots: a grey scrap high for wind days, a white scrap low for flood, twin loops to mean the bridge is live. The boy across copied each sign exactly, tongue caught between teeth.

Payment was equal and simple. Salt pinch out, skin thong in. La-la ran the thong through her fingers once. "Good," she said, which was a big word coming from her.

The old woman touched the line with one finger. "Now trade doesn’t fall in," she said, and sat down like that settled the matter.

At dusk, both banks tapped once--hold. Hakaka marked the ash ledger: line across, no feet, paid both, hum steady. She swept it clean.

The bridge sang all night in the small way. No one crossed. Everything reached.

Badge #28 -- Reed Pinch

Mark: white scrap low · leaf knot · two taps to heel
Vow: Send help, not hands.

Midday, the far boy limped. He stood where the blue scraps meet the stones and pointed at his heel. The woman held up a long thorn, then showed two fingers--two thorns, or deep.

They set the stones and showed empty hands. The cliff man raised his palm for quiet and kept the boy back from the lip.

Hakaka nodded once. "We can send the way, not our feet."

La-la split a fresh reed to its tip, shaped a narrow mouth with a twist--pinch. Ya-la boiled a leaf in the small pot and shook it cool. Sa-la cut a thin strip of bark, bitter.

They packed the pouch: the reed pinch, one clean leaf, one bitter strip, a flat shell for water. Ya-la added three pebbles and set them in a row--do this, then this, then this.

Signs across water:
	•	hand in a bowl shape, pour from shell: soak
	•	two fingers to the air, slow: count breaths
	•	reed pinch to heel: take it out
	•	leaf to skin, flat palm: press
	•	bark to mouth, grimace: chew small

Ya-la acted each step on Sa-la’s bare heel in the shallow edge where all could see. Soak. Count ten with slow fingers. Pinch and pull in one breath. Press the leaf. Chew a bit of bark. Sa-la did not flinch and looked pleased with himself. La-la tapped his ear. "Watch, not show."

Across, the woman held the boy’s foot in the shell. The cliff man counted with the same slow fingers. On eight the boy tried to hurry. The woman shook her head and held him through nine, ten. She took the reed pinch and set it true. One pull. The thorn lifted and showed itself. The boy hissed between his teeth, then laughed once, small and surprised. Blood welled. She pressed the leaf flat and kept it there. The cliff man tore a thread from his belt and tied the leaf in place with a square, honest knot.

They sent the bark strip back again with two pebbles--again, but small. The woman cut it into three tiny bites with a flake and fed the boy one. He made the bitter face. She made the same face so he would know it was fair.

Dispute drill, quiet: both sides showed empty hands; both sides touched heel and leaf to say the same thing.

Payment was simple. Hakaka dropped a pinch of ground salt in the run and opened her hand so the river could see she had nothing left to hide. Across, the woman set a small twist of blue thread into the pouch and tied the loop neat.

"Keep," Ya-la said, touching the reed pinch, then her chest. Across, the woman touched it to her own chest and nodded once--kept.

They lifted the stones at last light and set them down as before. Hakaka marked the ash ledger: thorn out across water, leaf held, no crossing, paid once. She swept it clean.

"Held," she said.

The blue scraps hung easy. The boy walked without the small wince. Both banks slept with feet quiet.

Badge #27 -- High Water Test

Mark: two stones · blue scrap · seal ring
Vow: Help without crossing.

Rain fell upriver in the night. By mid-morning the water had a new voice--fast, crowded. The blue scraps leaned toward each other and shook a little.

They set the stones again and showed empty hands. The woman and the boy did the same. The cliff man stood with the coil.

The center ring sat where they had left it--two turns, a bite, one tuck. The line to it began to hum.

Rules stayed hands:
	•	two tugs on the ring: pull together
	•	flat palm down: slack
	•	finger circle in the air: cut free
	•	palm to chest, then to water: pay the river

A basket from upstream slid sideways and hung on the seal line. It pinned the line into a hard angle. The ring chirped and turned wrong. The boy stepped too close; mud gave under his heel. He windmilled and found his feet again. The woman caught his shoulder. She did not let go.

"Together," Hakaka said. She raised both hands, then showed two fingers. Across, the cliff man nodded once and put his weight on the coil.

They pulled as if lifting a sleeping limb. The basket shifted a hand’s width, then shoved back and bit. The ring complained. La-la laid a palm down--slack. They gave the river breath. The line settled.

"Again," Ya-la said. Two fingers. Across, the boy copied her shape, then held it still like a promise. They pulled together on the next small wave. The basket walked a little and stuck again.

"Cut," Sa-la said, too fast. Hakaka shook her head once. Not yet.

The old woman coughed the short cough. "Shorten." La-la slid the stand a step into the lee of the rock. Across, the boy dragged their stake the same way. The ring eased half a finger and stopped spinning wrong.

"Pay," Hakaka said. Palm to chest, then to the water. Ya-la dropped three greens where the line met the current and opened her hand so the river could see. Across, the woman crumbled a pinch of dried meat and let it go. The crumbs vanished like they had work to do.

Two tugs. Together. The wave lifted; they matched it. The basket freed a thumb, then a palm, then swung. La-la turned her wrist. "Let it choose." Slack. The basket found the seam and went on, heavy and finished with them.

The ring spun twice, calmed, and sat true. The line stopped humming like a threat and hummed like work.

They checked the border: stones steady, scraps still. No one had crossed. The cliff man’s mouth moved once like a word stayed behind his teeth. The boy blew out a breath he had been holding and tried to make it nothing. The woman’s hand left his shoulder and tapped the seal knot twice--again, not today.

Dispute drill, practiced for truth: both sides opened their palms and showed they had paid. Hakaka added a thin strip of fat to the current anyway, not because the drill told her, but because it felt right to thank what had let go. Across, the woman nodded slow, then set a pebble by her stone. The boy copied and set one at the same height.

La-la pressed her palm to the wet line near the ring. "Alive," she said. Sa-la reached and stopped himself. He let the feeling stay with her.

They lifted the stones at last light and set them down again so the ground would know nothing had changed. Hakaka marked the ash ledger: ring held under flood, no cuts, paid twice. She swept the marks in.

"Held," she said.

Across the water the blue scraps hung quiet. The border kept its work by being simple and kept its promise by not breaking under need. Both banks slept light and did not dream of crossing.

Badge #26 -- Quiet Border

Mark: two stones · blue scrap
Vow: Step soft where talk lives.

They set two flat stones at the river lip, one on each bank, the width of a palm between them and the water. Ya-la tied a strip of blue to a reed and stuck it behind their stone so the scrap would not touch the river, only point at it. Across, the woman did the same. The boy smoothed mud around their stone with the side of his hand.

"Inside the stones, no hard edges," Hakaka said. They laid knives, hooks, and awls on a cloth behind the line. Across, the cliff man showed his empty hands, turned them, and set a bone spike on his side of the cloth.

Rules were hands, not speeches:
	•	palms up: safe
	•	fist to heart: thanks
	•	three taps on the reed: stop
	•	two taps: wait
	•	one tap: again

La-la drew a shallow groove from their stone to the beam stand and put a small pebble in the groove. "Walk the groove," she said. Sa-la tested a step too wide; the old woman clicked her tongue once. He stepped on the groove after that.

They made a shared knot for the center ring--two turns, a bite, one tuck. Ya-la tied it slow where all could see. Across, the woman tied the same knot, not looking away. "Seal," Hakaka said. Two hands to loosen, two to tie. They touched wrists over the ring and let go at the same time.

Wind picked up and pushed talk sideways. Taro shifted the stand into the wind shadow of a rock; across, the boy did the same without words. "Shelter the rule," Ya-la said, soft. The beam lay still.

Dispute drill: if the beam would not agree after three tries, both sides added a small share to the water--greens or fish flakes--palms open so the river could see. Then they tied the seal again and started tomorrow. They practiced it once so the river would remember too.

A cough from the old woman meant "shorten," a raised finger meant "you missed the count," a flat hand meant "enough." She used all three in the next hour and nobody argued. The work went quiet and straight.

At last light, they set the cloth back over the tools, lifted the stones with both hands, and placed them down again to show the ground stayed the same. Across, the cliff man did the same with care that did not fit his size.

Hakaka pressed a line of four dots into the ash ledger: stone, scrap, knot, taps. She swept them in with the side of her hand. "Held," she said.

Across the water, the blue scraps did not move. The river spoke only in small sounds. The border did its work by not needing to be loud.

Badge #25 -- Fair Weight

Mark: reed beam · three stones
Vow: Weigh before want.

They needed a way to call "enough" without arguing. Hakaka said, "Make a balance." La-la cut a straight reed, split the middle a finger-width. Taro found a forked stick for a stand. Sa-la drilled two tiny holes at the beam ends with a hot thorn and didn’t burn them bigger the second time. Ya-la tied two bark cups with equal cord and checked that they hung the same. The beam tipped left, then right, then found the middle when Hakaka slid a pebble along the split.

"Stone marks center," Hakaka said. "Don’t move it unless all eyes see." The old woman tapped the stand. "Level first," she said. La-la wedged a flake under one foot until the beam lay flat and quiet.

They made three weight-stones: one heavy, one middle, one seed-small. Ya-la cut three short notches on the heavy, two on the middle, one on the small. "Speak plain," she said. They tested with water, filling Carrier to the scratch, then splitting by halves. The beam agreed twice. "Good," La-la said. "Again." It agreed a third time.

Across the river they raised the blue scrap and showed the beam. The woman with the scar lifted their own reed, rough but true enough. The cliff man set his palm under their beam and waited. Hakaka nodded. "Trade by weight," she said, and held up one notch.

First measure: dried greens for fish bone. Ya-la set the middle stone on their side, poured greens till the beam kissed level. Sa-la tried to slick a thumb on the string; Hakaka’s eyes stopped him. Across, the woman added bones to match; the beam breathed, then stilled. The boy grinned. The cliff man did not.

Second measure: clay for salt. La-la set the heavy stone and a small together. The woman shook her head, held up two fingers, then pointed to her arm: thin. Hakaka slid the small stone off. They tried again. The beam found center; both sides kept their faces straight and their hands careful.

A wind pushed. The beam drifted. Taro cupped a hand to block, and Ya-la moved the stand a step behind a rock. "Shelter the rule," she said. The old woman snorted. "Now it lives," she said.

They cut a small ring from reed and tied it under the center notch with a knot all of them could name. "Seal," Hakaka said. "Two hands to loosen, two to tie." She touched La-la’s knuckles; La-la touched Ya-la’s. Sa-la kept his fingers behind his back on purpose and watched.

When they were done, Hakaka pressed three dots into the ash line--heavy, middle, small--and swept them in. "Ledger," she said. "Fair weight." Sa-la set the stones back in a pouch and hung it high, where wanting would have to climb.

That night the stew tasted like there would be more tomorrow. The old woman ate slow and said nothing, which meant right. Sa-la lay on his back and counted the notches in his head until sleep counted the rest.

Across the river, a reed beam flashed once in the last light, then went still. The line lay quiet. The rule held.

Badge #24 -- Salt Measure

Mark: bone spoon · hole tie
Vow: Take only a fair pinch.

The salt twist sat in Ya-la’s palm like a kept spark. Hakaka said, "Jar." Taro brought clay. La-la pinched a small pot with a tight mouth. Sa-la rolled a plug for a lid. "Flat," Hakaka said. "No cracks." He turned it until it stopped wobbling.

They fired the jar by the coals. It thunked true when La-la tapped it with a stick. Ya-la put two grains on her tongue, nodded once, and tipped the rest in. "Measure," Hakaka said.

La-la held a rib bone. "Spoon," she said. Sa-la burned a small hollow with a coal on a stick, blew, scraped it smooth with a flake. Taro rubbed it in sand until it didn’t catch skin. Ya-la tied a loop of reed at the end so it wouldn’t wander.

"How much?" Sa-la asked. Hakaka pointed at the stew pot. "Six mouths," she said. Ya-la dipped the spoon once, leveled it with a finger, dropped the pinch. She stirred. They waited. The old woman sipped first, rolled it in her mouth, and grunted. "Food," she said. Ya-la added one more spoon, no level. The old woman’s eyes cut sideways. "Enough," she said. "Rule," Hakaka said. "One level spoon for six. Taste. Stop."

They made a second spoon. Smaller. "For only us," Ya-la said. Sa-la drilled the hole too near the edge and it cracked. He clenched, then picked another rib and started again, slower. The dog laid its head on his foot and slept. The second spoon lived.

Ya-la braided a thin hanger for the big spoon and tied it above the pot place with a reef knot. "High," La-la said. "So hands think first." Sa-la reached up and touched the knot, then put his hand down empty.

They wrapped the small spoon in leaf with a pinch of salt and a blue tie. Hakaka set it in the reed pouch. "Send," she said. The line hummed as La-la paid it out. Sa-la slid the pouch low, calm. Across, the woman with the scar lifted it, looked at the hole-tied handle, and tasted one level pinch on her own tongue. She nodded once. She put in a bundle of dried greens and a short woven loop. The man watched her hands and did not speak. The boy leaned too far and the man’s hand found his shoulder and set it back.

The pouch came home. La-la opened the greens. Bitter, then good. "Stew," Ya-la said. She hung the woven loop by the jar mouth. "Their hanger," she said. Hakaka set one grain on the ash line, pressed it with her thumb, and swept it into the floor. "Ledger," she said. "Fair pinch."

They ate. The old woman said nothing more, which meant right. Taro stacked the extra rib scraps neat. Sa-la stood by the new jar with his hands behind him until the wanting left his fingers.

Before dark, Hakaka tied a short knot in the signal cord. "Measure holds us," she said. La-la touched it. Ya-la touched it. Sa-la touched it and then wiped his salty thumb on his own shirt, not the robe. Across the river, a small spoon flashed once in the last light, then hung still. The line lay quiet. The rule stayed.

Badge #23 -- Bridge Meal

Mark: clay wrap · blue knot
Vow: Share first bite.

The fish from the weir lay cool on stone. La-la gutted clean, quick. Ya-la rinsed the bellies and set them aside for broth. Sa-la fetched flat leaves. Taro carried clay in a cracked bowl.

"Thin," Hakaka said. "No cracks." Taro nodded and pressed the clay flat with his palm, edges neat. They wrapped two fish in leaf and clay, pinched the seams, marked each with a thumb press so they’d know which was which.

Stones reddened in the coals. La-la rolled the hot ones out with sticks. The clay bundles went on. Stones over. Then ash. Then a ring of smaller rocks to keep the heat honest.

"Don’t poke," Hakaka said. Sa-la folded his arms to sit on them and grinned without showing teeth. The old woman sniffed, said nothing. The dog lay where the smoke didn’t bite.

They waited. The waiting tried Sa-la first. Ya-la put a hand on his knee. "Breathe," she said. He did. The smell turned from wet to food.

"Now," La-la said. They pulled the caps. Clay cracked easy. Steam pushed out. Fish came loose in white sheets.

Hakaka cut one clean in thirds: old woman, La-la, Ya-la. Sa-la reached and stopped himself. Hakaka handed him the spare clay cap. "Carry," she said.

They wrapped the second fish tighter, tied a blue knot on the leaf cord, and set it in the basket pouch. Sa-la walked to the reed line. "Low and easy," Hakaka said. He nodded, set his feet, and slid the pouch out over the water. The current took it, slow and sure, like it remembered school.

Across, the boy came down first, then the woman, then the cliff of a man. The woman lifted the pouch, checked the tie, looked up. Ya-la held up her own piece and took a bite so they would see the rule. The woman did the same, small and neat. The boy copied big and fast; the man waited and then nodded once and ate.

They chewed with the river. No talk. Just the sound of it.

When they were done, the pouch came back with the leaf tie folded tighter and a small twist of salt inside. La-la tasted a grain and blinked. "Good sharp," she said. Hakaka tipped the twist into Ya-la’s palm. "Ledger," she said. Ya-la touched the ash floor with two fingers and then her chest. "In us," she said. "Underfoot," the old woman added, because she had to.

Sa-la licked the clay off his thumbs and looked at the line. "Tomorrow?" he asked. Hakaka shook her head. "Next fish, first bite still theirs," she said. Across, as if hearing, the woman lifted the blue knot and tied it to her wrist with the boy’s scrap. The man set his palm to his chest and raised it a little, the smallest thanks.

They banked the coals, rinsed the stones, and set the cracked clay pieces in a pile for patching. Taro stacked the good ones by size. The dog licked a leaf and sighed.

Before dark, Hakaka retied the signal cord and touched the knot. "Share first bite," she said. La-la touched it. Ya-la touched it. Sa-la touched it. Across the river, three hands did the same. Then the night took the light down, and the line lay quiet between banks like a promise that didn’t need more words.

Badge #22 -- Twin Weir

Mark: forked stake · blue/white strip
Vow: Leave one gap.

Morning ran steady and low. Taro waded to the thigh with a forked pole and felt for hard bottom. "Here," he said. Across, the cliff man stepped out to match him, slow and sure. They pointed with their poles until both tips agreed.

La-la set the first stake. She leaned it a hair upstream. "So the pull makes it truer," she said. Sa-la held the lash. Ya-la kept the coil clean. Hakaka picked two hammer stones, one for each bank, same weight in the hand.

They tied the law to the peg where anyone could grab it: one knot--set; two knots--brace; red bead--stop. Ya-la slotted a white scrap beside the blue on the signal strip. "Blue means river. White means stone. Both means both hands," she said. The boy across copied, tongue out, careful.

They drove the first pair together. La-la’s stone spoke three times; across, the cliff man answered three. The stakes sat. The line between them took a soft curve and stopped arguing. "Next," Hakaka said.

A shallow throat took shape: stakes in a V that pointed downstream toward an open mouth where a basket would live. Taro split reeds with a rib sliver and twisted fast. Sa-la tried his speed and dropped a lash. La-la didn’t look at him. "Slow wins," she said. He slowed. The lash held.

Midday, the river showed a trick. One stake drifted as if thinking better of the job. Ya-la lifted two fingers: brace. Both sides slid their palms down the stake line and leaned, not hard, just enough to teach it sense. The line went quiet again.

The woman across showed a faster tie--half-knot, twist, tuck. She did it without pride. Sa-la copied. His first tuck slipped; his second did not. He did not look up for praise. The dog watched a dragonfly and pretended not to care.

They left the vow space. Hakaka set a smooth, round stone in the mouth where a fish could slip through if it chose right. She held it up. Across, the woman held up her own stone, near the same size. Both set them and did not speak about it.

They seated the basket. Reed throat, tight weave, mouth tied to the inside stakes with two loops that would give a little and no more. La-la tested it with her fist and then with a tug. "Good," she said. "It won’t lie."

First test: a silver body nosed the V, felt the basket mouth, and went in because it didn’t know better. The throat narrowed behind it. Taro moved to lift; Hakaka shook her head once. "Let it calm," she said. They waited till the fish stopped writing panic. Then La-la lifted the basket just enough for water to keep speaking around it.

Ya-la slid her hand in and took the small one, not the largest. She turned and showed the others: alive, quick. She set it free through the vow gap. Across, the boy grinned, full teeth. The cliff man gave one slow nod. The old woman coughed and said, "Good manners," like she was tired of being right.

They emptied the basket proper. Three fat fish went to shore--one laid on a cool stone for trade, two for stew. Ya-la tied a blue strip to the trade one. Sa-la reset the throat, hands steady now.

Wind freshened. Floats ticked the line wrong. Hakaka pointed. "Wider by one hand." La-la slid them out. The ticking stopped.

Near dusk they walked the weir once, each stake with a palm on it, checking for wobble and lies. A soft place sucked at La-la’s heel. "Mark it," she said. Taro cut a notch in the nearest stake, waist-high, so a tired body would see it even in bad light.

They left the pegs to keep watch again. Ya-la hung the signal cord where night hands could find it. Across, the woman did the same. Sa-la set the spare stones under their peg so no one would trip in hurry. The dog chose a dry patch and sighed like he’d built it.

Before they went, Hakaka touched the vow stone. "Still a gap," she said. La-la touched it too. Ya-la did. Sa-la did. Across the river, four hands did the same. Then the light went thin, and the weir hummed its small, work sound, and both banks went to make fire.

Badge #21 -- Quiet Bridge

Mark: peg pair · blue notch
Vow: Agree before pull.

Morning gave them a steady seam. Taro set a peg high on their bank where the line ran true. Across, the cliff man answered with a peg opposite, slow and exact. They didn’t tie a rope across; they let the water keep its spine.

La-la shaved thin floats from reed and slid them on the pouch line like beads, one hand apart. "Shows where it rides," she said. Sa-la counted the gaps with his thumb. "Don’t lose the count," La-la said, not unkind.

Ya-la cut a short strip and marked one end with a single blue notch. "Throw," she said, tapping the notch. She tied two notches on another. "Wait." She rubbed a red bead with ash and set it beside both. "Danger." She did it slow so her hands would remember. Hakaka watched and said nothing.

They sent the first pouch light--two roots, a sliver of smoked fat, the "throw" strip. It rode, dipped, chose the inside curve, and came to the woman. The woman held up her own strip: two notches. She didn’t take the pouch. Ya-la lifted her hand, palm down. Wait. The boy pointed at a back-eddy farther down. A branch rolled in it like a lazy animal.

La-la nodded. "If we pull now it snags." She pinched the line, felt the seam talking. "It’ll clear in three breaths." They breathed with it. On the third breath the branch slipped its sulk and went on. The woman dropped her strip, lifted one notch, once. Throw. Sa-la smiled with his mouth closed. La-la eased the line. The woman took the pouch then, as agreed. She put in three small fish, bright still, and a thumb of salt in leaf, tight-wrapped. She sent it back with the "wait" strip and the red bead on top, to say both sides knew why.

They tested the "pull" itself. Hakaka tied a dull stone in another pouch and slid it out until it kissed the seam’s shoulder and thought about sticking. She looked up. The woman had already set one foot back and braced. Ya-la raised two fingers, closed them, opened--together. Both sides leaned, not hard, just enough to speak to the snag. The pouch shivered loose and came on. No one slipped. The old woman’s mouth twitched; she pretended it was the smoke.

They wrote the rule plain. La-la knotted a short cord: one knot near the end, throw; two knots apart, wait; two knots pinched together, pull together. Ya-la added the red bead on a loop that slid. She hung it by their peg where hands would find it in the dark. Across, the woman did the same with different cord and same law. The boy touched both and laughed once, small.

Wind turned and lied for a while. The floats began to chatter, bumping the line wrong. Sa-la reached to snatch the near float; Hakaka caught his wrist. "Watch first." The chatter settled into a new count: slower, farther apart. "Now," Hakaka said. La-la slid the floats wider by one hand. The line went quiet again. Sa-la grinned at the simple fix and didn’t say a word. He moved the spare floats to match and checked the count without being told. The dog lay on his feet and agreed by staying.

Near noon a shadow came--otter or something like it--sleek and certain. It nosed the pouch, curious and rude. Across the water the boy hissed; Sa-la nearly hissed back. Ya-la lifted the red bead high and then put it down on the bank beside her knee: danger, but not for pulling. They all stilled. The animal lost interest and wrote nothing, slid on. The old woman coughed once and called it stupid under her breath like a blessing.

They closed the day with one last exchange: Ya-la sent their "wait" strip back across on purpose. The woman tied their two-notch to her own peg with a simple knot, no making it pretty. The cliff man looked at both pegs and gave one short nod. Taro carved a notch across the tops of both their pegs, same angle, so even the moon would read it.

Hakaka stood a moment with her palm on their peg, feeling the river think. "Bridge without weight," she said. La-la checked the floats one more time. Ya-la covered the salt leaf so no one salted their tongue for want of sense. Sa-la tied the spare cord to the peg base and tugged it once, firm and honest.

At dusk the far camp hung their two-knot cord where wet light could find it. Ya-la lifted theirs to match. Both sides let the pegs keep watch. The wind shifted again and tried to confuse the floats. The floats told the truth anyway.

Badge #20 -- Hook-Blue

Mark: blue twist · ash water
Vow: Color without waste; signal without lies.

They made lye first. Ya-la poured warm water through last night’s ash into a clay cup until it felt slick between two fingers. "Not strong," she said. "Just enough to wake it."

Hakaka unrolled the blue twist on a flat stone. It bled a pale green that wanted to hide. Sa-la leaned in with the cup. "Slow," La-la told him, and moved his hand a thumb’s width. Ya-la dripped lye a drop at a time. The wet turned the green darker, not blue yet. "Air," Hakaka said. They waited and watched it breathe into color. It did. The old woman nodded once like a debt paid on time.

They needed a thread that would keep it. Taro split a dry reed and teased out the strong skin. Sa-la twisted two skins together, polite this time. Ya-la dipped the cord, wrung it with her palm, lifted it to the air. It came up green, then shifted as the wind thought about it. Blue found it like river light. Sa-la grinned and looked down fast so it wouldn’t feel chased.

Across the bend, the far people watched. The scar-mouth woman stood with her hands behind her back so her shoulders wouldn’t talk. The boy shaded his eyes. The cliff man set a small pot near their hump and didn’t touch it again.

La-la prepped three more cords. "Test," she said. One they dipped and lifted. One they dipped, dried, dipped again. One they rubbed with fat first. The fat one sulked and took nothing. "Not for coats," La-la said. "Skin before color and color goes hungry." Sa-la held the failed cord anyway because it felt good.

They tried cloth. Ya-la cut a thin strip from the robe’s spare hem and tied it to a peg, not the robe itself. "We don’t learn on what keeps us," Hakaka said. Ya-la dipped the strip, wrung, lifted. The blue climbed from the edges in. "Two breaths," Ya-la said to herself, and counted them out. She set the strip in shade. "Shade holds it," she said. "Sun steals quick." La-la moved the strip without being asked when the sun reached for it.

The old woman sucked the edge of her tooth and pointed at the ash cup. "Keep it covered," she said. "Air eats everything." Sa-la set a shard over it like a lid and put a small stone on top so his hand would remember.

They made signs. Hakaka laid a reed on the ground and tied two blue knots near one end. "With," she said, touching both knots. She tied one knot in the middle. "Again." Ya-la added a fish scale beside the middle knot. "River." La-la frowned, then nodded. "Not pretty. True." Sa-la tried to tie the middle knot blind, eyes closed; he got it after three tries. He left the wrong knots on the ground and did not kick them.

They sent a message. In the reed pouch they put the blue-knotted strip, two pebbles, a fish scale, a small pinch of oven paste wrapped in leaf. Hakaka looked at the river, then at Sa-la. "Flat and low," she said. He threw flat and low. The pouch rode a seam like it knew the way home better than either side.

The woman took it. She opened the leaf, tasted with the tip of her tongue like a cat that still had pride. She touched the blue knots and looked at their wrists. She spoke to the boy without moving her mouth. He ran and came back with a cord they’d dyed wrong--too long in the sun; it had gone dull. She did not throw it away. She tied it into the pouch with a hook wire and a small clay bead the color of dry blood. She added three pebbles, not two. She sent it back.

La-la poured the pebbles into her palm and sorted their meanings with her face. "Three?" Sa-la said. Ya-la tapped the bead. "Not food," she said. She put it to her nose. It smelled like river clay cooked too hot. She rubbed it on her wrist. It left a fat mark that didn’t move. "Mark," she said. She drew a thin blue line across the red. "Ours with theirs," Hakaka said. The old woman held out her hand. Ya-la marked her knuckle. The old woman didn’t look at it. "Don’t waste it," she said.

They made a thing to show and not shout. La-la shaved a flat of reed and bent it into a small circle, tied with a plain cord. Ya-la wove the blue strip through it: over, under, over, under, leaving the ends to flutter. "Wind door," La-la said, and hung it near the shelter mouth so you could read it from the river. Hakaka put one blue dot above the circle in ash and wiped it away, then put it again. "Tomorrow," she said. "No hiding."

Sa-la wanted his own bit. He asked with his eyes. Ya-la gave him the dull-blue strip. "Practice," she said. He tied three knots fast and wrong, then slower and right. He wore it around his wrist and did not show it to the far boy. The dog came and sniffed the strip. Sa-la let it and didn’t pretend to kick.

Work kept moving. They rinsed the lye cup, saved the blue water in a small pot, and tied a grass slip around its neck so no one drank it in the dark. Taro drove a thin peg at the bank where the seam ran truest. "Throw from here," he said to his own feet. The old woman tucked the green powder tight and forgot to cough for a little while.

Near dusk the far camp answered. They lifted a strip the color of river skin at evening--grey that wanted blue and didn’t dare. It hung from a stick planted in the wet. The woman tapped her chest once and then held up three fingers, dropped one, held two. Ya-la answered with two fingers and a flat hand--same, stay. The cliff man stood behind her like he was part of the bank. The boy copied Sa-la’s wrist strip knot without the strip and laughed once, quiet.

Night pulled in the edges. Hakaka tied the blue strip high on the shelter post. La-la moved the wind door a hand toward leeward so it wouldn’t lift in the wrong breath and lie. Ya-la set the lye cup under a pot so children and Sa-la’s hands wouldn’t find it first. Taro made a notch in the bank peg so he could find it blind. The old woman turned her marked knuckle toward the fire when she fell asleep.

"Story?" Sa-la asked. "Small," Hakaka said. "We found a color. We didn’t chase it. It stayed." She tucked the shard over the blue pot’s mouth and put a pebble on it with two fingers. "Tomorrow," she said, and left the pebble there to say it twice.

Badge #19 -- Field Oven

Mark: ring of stone · small draw
Vow: Wait until the wait turns sweet.

Morning had a bite. Breath showed. Hakaka chose a flat patch above the flood line and paced a circle with her heel. "Here," she said. "Wind comes from there. Slope pulls that way."

La-la scored the circle with a stick. "Two hands wide beyond the biggest thing we’ll cook," she said. Taro measured with his palm. Sa-la brought armfuls of dry grass and the flattest stones. Ya-la filled the water jug and set it in shade. The old woman sat where the sun would get her knees and kept her cough in a tight fist.

They dug shallow first: a bowl of earth as deep as La-la’s wrist. Taro cut a small draw trench from the rim to the downslope--a mouth for air, not greedy. "Heat likes tight," La-la said. She lined the bowl with stones that didn’t ring when tapped; ringers crack. Hakaka thumbed clay into the seams. "No runaway breath."

Sa-la started the first fire in the naked pit, twigs, dry grass, a few thicker sticks. He watched the smoke choose the draw trench. "Good," Hakaka said. "Let the stones learn warm."

They built the dome while the stones drank heat. Ya-la mixed mud with chopped straw and the smallest reeds, kneading until it held a shape when she squeezed it. La-la made a ring of field stones around the pit and bridged them with flat pieces, leaving a hole at the top you could cover with your palm. "It needs a lid that obeys," she said. Taro fetched a broken pot shard that fit like it had been waiting.

Sa-la reached to slap on mud too thick. La-la caught his wrist. "Thin skins first," she said. "Thick falls." He nodded, started again. Thin coat, then another after it quit shining. They smoothed with wet hands until the dome looked like a sleeping animal. Hakaka pressed a small coin of clay over the draw mouth’s rim to remember the wind.

Across the river the far people watched from their bend. The scar-mouthed woman crossed her arms. The boy pointed once; the cliff-shouldered man looked at the sky like it owed him something. Ya-la lifted a hand without waving. "After," she murmured.

The first fire went to coals. Hakaka raked them to the edges so the belly stayed hot and the mud skin didn’t cry. Little cracks spoke. Ya-la smeared them shut. "Quiet," she told the clay. The dome steamed in places you could see. La-la moved her palm near the little top hole and felt the speed the heat wanted. "Cover," she said. Taro set the shard in place. The sound inside changed, low and pleased.

They fed the oven again--small sticks, then thicker, never forcing. Sa-la bent to blow too eager and got a mouthful of ash. He coughed, spat dark, and grinned without pride. "Slow mouth," Ya-la said. He nodded, eyes watering.

Hakaka pulled the heat when the dome turned from wet-dark to dry-pale. They scraped coals into a trench and sealed the draw with a plug of wet grass and clay. The belly of the oven held breath. "Now we wait," Hakaka said.

Waiting had work. La-la sliced roots thin and dusted them with salt. Ya-la worked a paste of ground seeds and water with the little pestle, pushing and folding until it listened. Taro pounded a flat of clay into a tray and dried it by the fire that wasn’t the oven’s fire. Sa-la checked the rim seam three times, because he didn’t trust himself not to bump it. He didn’t bump it.

The old woman watched all this like a judge with a full book. "If it falls," she said, "we eat smoke." "We won’t," La-la answered, not looking up.

They opened the shard lid with cloth around fingers. Heat kissed the air and tried to run. Hakaka let it, just a little. "Feed," she said. The tray went in with the seeds paste spread hand-thick and the root slices on a flat stone beside it. Shard back. "Now we wait right," Hakaka said.

Waiting right meant not peeking. It meant ears and nose instead of eyes. At first they smelled mud and yesterday. Then the smell shifted. Sa-la’s shoulders lowered. "Sweet," he said, surprised. The old woman’s mouth tilted. "Not yet," she warned.

Across the water, a thin shout skimmed the surface. The scar-mouthed woman cupped her hands and sent a sound like two pebbles struck and a long breath. Ya-la lifted a strip of blue and let it hang from her wrist. "Not yet," she echoed, pointing at the dome and the sky in one small line. The boy across laughed without a sound anyone could hear.

A crack webbed the dome near the top hole, the kind that means "enough" not "broken." La-la touched it and felt the heat say yes. "Open," she said. Hakaka took the shard. The smell rose like a soft hand. They pulled the tray and the root stone with sticks. The paste had browned at the edges and blistered where the bubble wanted to be a mouth. The roots had gone from stubborn to willing.

Sa-la reached. La-la’s look stopped him closer than a hand could. "Air first," she said. They waited until the skin quit spitting and the color deepened one shade. Hakaka broke a corner. Steam spoke in a language everyone knew. She gave the old woman the first bite. "About time," the old woman said after chewing it until it quit arguing.

They ate without talking, because hot food has its own conversation. The paste was nut-sweet, a little smoke, a little ash from the edge. The roots were tender with a clean bite. Ya-la wiped her thumb over the tray to chase the last oil and didn’t lick it until she saw Sa-la watching. He smirked and then didn’t.

A dog came, thin and curious. Taro had a stone in his sling before the thought finished. He let it thrum once without release. The dog read the music and kept distance. Ya-la threw a peel that wasn’t food far away and the dog went to re-learn disappointment.

Across the river, smoke rose from a little hump near the far camp. The woman had copied the dome wrong at first--too thick, too hungry a draw. The cliff man knocked the side and the top slid an inch. He didn’t swear. He pressed it back and sealed it with mud. The boy watched, face lit like first fire. Ya-la lifted the shard in her hand and made a circle with two fingers: thin skins. The woman snorted, the sound carrying just enough to be a joke between banks.

When the oven cooled enough to touch, Hakaka tapped the skin here and there. "Lives," she said. She showed Sa-la where to smear the little mouth with clay when the wind forgot itself. She showed Taro how to clear the throat without stealing its shape. La-la made a small wad of grass to keep the shard from ringing and set it beside the hole like a lid’s pillow. Ya-la took a piece of charcoal and drew a round with a small mouth and a smaller mouth on the ash floor near the oven: dome, draw, breathe. Two dots at the rim meant "thin skins." She wiped it clean with her palm and pressed the soot into the floor with the heel of her hand.

They kept a little of the paste for the far side and wrapped it in a leaf that didn’t leak. Hakaka set it in the reed pouch with two pebbles and a fish scale--"with" and "again" and "river" in their new grammar--and handed it to Sa-la. "Easy," she said. He didn’t run. He set the throw flat and low and watched the line, not his own hands.

The pouch drifted to the far bank. The boy stepped and then remembered to wait. The woman lifted the leaf, broke a corner, put it on her tongue. She didn’t change her face. She set another piece in the boy’s hand. The cliff man smelled and gave nothing away. The woman put a flat pinch of ground leaf into the pouch, green and sharp, and a twist of something blue that bled into the leaf like river under ice. She tied the loop better than Sa-la had tied it and sent it back with one small push.

Ya-la untied the blue twist and her fingers came away faintly colored. "Not for eating," she said, smiling with her eyes. The green powder bit the nose and opened the head behind the eyes. The old woman sniffed it and stopped coughing for the space of eight breaths. She handed it back without comment. "Trade," Hakaka said, and no one argued.

They sealed the oven mouth with a clay coin and a flat stone. "Not tight," La-la said. "Let night walk through." Taro set a small line of pebbles beside the dome to show the wind’s road for morning. Sa-la pressed his thumb into the dome’s side once, a soft, private mark, and then rubbed it out with the heel of his hand.

As the light leaned away, the far dome gave a small pop and kept itself. The boy clapped once and the woman pretended not to have heard. The cliff man looked at the river and nodded like it had finally said something sensible.

They cleaned what needed it and left what would teach them tomorrow. Ya-la hung the shard on a peg with a loop of reed. Hakaka moved the water jug into shadow again because it was a habit that saved thinking later. La-la checked the draw mouth one last time and pushed a finger-width of grass into the gap. "Sleep," she told the oven, like you tell a child who listens.

"Story?" Sa-la asked. The old woman lay back and stared at the small blue left on Ya-la’s thumb. "Smallest," she said. Hakaka sat and let her hands warm on her knees. "We were hungry," she said. "We learned slow. We waited right. It fed us." She stopped. That was enough.

Night came. The dome kept a little hum you could only hear if you put your ear against it and forgot pride. Taro took first watch without being told. The dog circled and lay down where it could smell both camps and dream fair.

In the morning, the dome was just dirt that knew a new trick. The ash drawing was gone. The waiting stayed.

Badge #18 -- First Weir

Mark: stone V · leader gap
Vow: Let it tire.

The river ran a little lower after three dry days. Stones showed their backs like slow fish. Hakaka stood where the water split around a knee of rock and tapped it with a stick. "Here," she said. "It already chooses."

La-la rolled her sleeves and stepped in to ankle depth. The cold climbed her shins like it meant to live there. "Line the push," she said, and set the first stone where the current helped hold it. Taro brought more, testing each against his thigh so it wouldn’t wobble. He laid them with the flattest face into the flow, tight, no gaps that could argue.

Sa-la threw one, splash and pride. The stone skated, hit La-la’s shin, and bit her. She didn’t swear. She picked it up and pressed it into place until it stopped thinking it was a river thing and remembered it was a wall thing. She looked at Sa-la. He nodded, small. He carried the next one with both hands.

They built a V with its open mouth downstream, the point aimed toward the knee of rock. Hakaka worked at the point with her hands, slow and exact, rubbing mud into the seams like law. "If the point is greedy," she said, "the river will punish it." Ya-la held the basket on her hip and watched how the eddies braided. She folded that into memory where she kept people and weather.

The old woman sat on the bank with the blanket high and her feet bare to the sun. "Catch a fat one," she said, like a queen. Sa-la grinned at her and then kept his face straight when La-la glanced.

They left a narrow path of stones leading into the V, a thin throat that said come this way without shouting. Past the throat, La-la set a low gate of willow stakes and twine. Not tight. Honest. "Don’t scare them," she said. "It’s work, not a fight." Sa-la wove a loose lattice with reed. His first bands crossed wrong and made a proud X that would snag everything. La-la pressed a finger to it. "This is where it will fail." He cut and threaded again. It learned to bend.

Across the water, the far people watched from the bend where sand made a small beach. The woman with the thin scar stood with her hands in her belt. The boy held the blue scrap, quiet this time. The cliff-shouldered man was a tree. Ya-la lifted her chin to them. She did not wave. Work first.

When the V reached mid-calf depth and the point held without complaint, they guided a branch into the throat and let the river push against it. The branch rubbed the stones and spoke in small, wet sounds. "Hear it?" Hakaka asked. Sa-la tilted his head. "It’s telling on the gaps." He moved a stone and the sound eased.

By noon, the weir took shape: two arms gathering water into the throat, a pocket behind the gate where tired fish might think they had a choice. La-la placed three stones near the mouth of the pocket, not in a row, not neat. "They like to hide behind trouble," she said. Taro laughed once under his breath. Ya-la caught it and kept it as a bright thing for later.

They ate cold tuber and a thin strip of meat on the bank. The old woman didn’t ask; Ya-la put a slice on a tile and slid it to her toe. "Too salty," the old woman said, eating.

When they stood again, the river had changed its mind in small ways. The left arm pushed harder, the right grew polite. "Shift," Hakaka said. She and La-la moved six stones as if they were one. The throat narrowed a thumb. Sa-la lifted a rock too big for him and made it look easy until it wasn’t. He grunted, set it, and then sat with the water running cold over his knees. "I will like this later," he announced. Ya-la smiled at the top of her mouth.

They set the basket downstream of the pocket, tied to a willow with a line that had already proved it knew how to return. Hakaka rubbed a little clay into the basket’s mouth so it would receive without bruising. "Soft," she said. "Let them give up without shame."

The first silver backs nosed the arms of the V while the sun leaned west. They came like thoughts--one, then none, then three at once. They tried the throat, felt the narrowing, and pushed harder because pushing is what a body knows. The weir didn’t stop them; it suggested.

A flash slapped the gate and slid along it. The lattice wavered and held. The fish turned and found the pocket and stayed because the pocket told a small lie about safety. Sa-la’s hands tightened on the line. Hakaka touched his wrist. "Wait until the wait turns sweet," she said. He breathed out. He held.

Two fish sat in the pocket, flanks talking light. La-la stepped in gentle, feet slow, and sank her hands behind them. One bolted and thumped the gate. The willow flexed and didn’t break. The other brushed La-la’s fingers and didn’t understand it had been chosen until it was on the bank and not in the world. She struck its head once, right. Ya-la whispered thanks without telling anyone she did.

The basket rocked and took a third, smaller fish as if it had decided to help. Sa-la whooped, swallowed it, and looked at Hakaka. She let a corner of her mouth go. "Now," she said. He pulled the basket in and set it where the water could still talk but not argue. He touched the gills with a wet hand to honor the air it used not to breathe.

"Leave one," Hakaka said. "Always one." Sa-la looked at the pocket and looked at her. La-la answered for him. "Or the river keeps its own tax with interest." Sa-la nodded, this time because he understood and not to prove he could.

They worked an hour: take one, free one, mend the reed when it scolded, shift a stone when the sound went wrong. The cliff-shouldered man on the far bank watched without moving. Once, the woman cupped both hands to her mouth and sent a flat shout that skated the top of the current. It was not a word they knew. Ya-la raised the bone hook they’d traded and touched it to her forehead and the river and then back to the basket, slow, a line with three knots. The woman tipped her chin: understood.

A dog came from upstream, ribs telling its own story. It skulked near the bank and thought about the basket. Taro stood, put a stone in his sling, and let the sling remind him, once around, twice, release--stone kissed the water hard in front of the dog’s nose. The dog decided it had urgent business elsewhere. The old woman grunted approval like a small drum with a cloth over it.

When the light went to copper, they eased the lattice open and let the pocket empty. Three fish slid out and went where the river always goes. Sa-la watched the last one and made himself not reach. He watched it flick and vanish into the dark line. "Go tell," he said, not loud. Ya-la heard and kept that too.

They carried the basket up to the flat stones. Hakaka chose the two larger fish for eating and one small one for tomorrow’s promise--guts buried near the reed bed, not underfoot. "Where we want more," she said. La-la cleaned fast and neat. Sa-la fetched water and didn’t spill even once. Taro cut thin, true slices with the sharp bone he had made to pay a debt and kept to honor it. The old woman held the salt horn and watched like a priest.

Across the river, the far people built their own V of stones a little wider, a little wrong at the point. Ya-la pointed with her chin, once. La-la shook her head no--work here. Hakaka made no sign. The woman across looked at their hands and changed one stone. The point softened. She did not look up. "Friends after," Ya-la said again, this time audible. No one argued.

They cooked on the field oven’s warm lip because it was still holding the day. Fish skin tightened and sang. The smell brought the dog back to the edge of memory and then sent it away again when it read the air for stones in slings. They ate with wet fingers and nobody spoke with their mouth full because that was a law now and not a courtesy.

When the bones showed clean, Ya-la wrapped them in a leaf and hung them on a branch. "High," Hakaka said. "Dogs have lessons too." Sa-la stood in the shallows and rubbed the slime from his forearms with sand. He looked at the weir in the last light. "We made a trap that respects," he said. La-la snorted soft. "We made a suggestion the river agreed with," she corrected. Hakaka put a hand on both their shoulders. "If it keeps working, it’s a law," she said. "If not, it was a good day."

They broke the throat open before full dark. Stones back enough that the river would not teach them a night-lesson about pride. The arms slouched, still there but not bossy, and the knee of rock took the push again. The water seemed to forgive them for trying.

On the bank, Ya-la drew a short V in ash near the oven, point softened, a dot beside it for the place they left empty on purpose. She smudged both and blew them away so the ledger would live underfoot and not in a bundle. Taro set the sling on a peg where his eyes would hit it at waking. Sa-la tied a reed ring around his wrist to remind himself which gap he always left too wide.

The old woman shifted to her side and tucked her cold feet under the blanket made of old rope-ends and mistakes. "Tomorrow," she said. "Do it again. Better." Her voice didn’t ask.

Night put its hand on the river and pressed. The knee of rock did its old work without praise. The basket dripped once, then stopped. Hakaka listened until she heard only the sounds that meant rest. "Watch," she said, and Taro touched his chest because it was his turn and he was ready.

In the grey before morning, a fish nosed where a throat had been and found nothing to push against but water. It went on. The river said what it always says. The V on the floor was gone. The lesson stayed.

Badge #16 -- Ash Ledger

Mark: palm-stripe in ash · pebble tally
Vow: Remember without weight.

Morning, they swept the floor clean of last night. Hakaka drew a wide stripe of ash with her palm and blew once to settle it. "Ledger," she said. "Only today."

Ya-la pinched a little coal and made the first mark: one short line for water fetched. Taro set a small heap of pebbles by the door. Sa-la crouched too close. The old woman moved her cup to where his knee would hit it if he forgot himself. He did not.

"Rules first," Hakaka said. La-la spoke them while she pinched the ash flat:

-- Write what happened, not what should have.
-- One mark per work. No fat.
-- Pebbles for numbers larger than hands.
-- At dusk, we read. At dawn, we sweep.
-- Memory goes in backs and feet.

They began.

Ya-la: four lines under WATER.
La-la: two lines under ROPE (mended), one notch for NET (new knot learned).
Taro: three pebbles under WOOD.
Sa-la: one line under FIRE (kept), one cross under ERROR (fed too quick), a dot beside it for FIXED. He looked up. Hakaka nodded once. The old woman snorted, which meant counted.

Across the stripe, Hakaka set DEBT / GIFT. She put down a meat-scrap’s shadow with ash for what came from the far bar last night, then a small ring for the clay plug they sent back. "Balance with food or work," she said. "Not with talk."

Midday, the ledger got crowded. The ash grew lumpy; marks blurred where ankles stepped. La-la used a reed tip to sharpen the lines. "Thin tells longer," she said. Ya-la marked FEVER with a single wavy stroke and then HONEY with two dots. The old woman watched and did not argue.

At WATCH, Hakaka wrote names in a column, each with a short tail in the direction they should face. Sa-la reached to put himself on first. Hakaka laid a finger on his wrist. "Second," she said. "You learn better when the edge is near but not yours." He nodded, which meant not yet angry.

A gust pushed at the door. The stripe shivered. Taro slid a flat stone across the threshold to keep footprints off the lower edge. "Stone remembers rules too," he said under breath. The old woman heard. "Stone forgets when dropped," she said. He grinned and didn’t drop it.

Afternoon brought the KILL / SPARE row--La-la’s snares had laughed and let go again. She marked a tuft of hair with three short scratches and tied a real tuft to the robe so the lesson would tug. "Ledger has a hand," Sa-la said, touching the pull. "Use both," Hakaka said.

Across the river, smoke curled different. Ya-la rubbed a shard of clay with charcoal and drew our stripe on it, then a small circle and two pebbles. She sent it by pouch. It came back with a notch cut on the shard’s edge and a line burned with a hot stick. Taro held it up. "Edge means count," he guessed. Ya-la set the shard on the ledger, cut our own notch with a flake, and made a matching burn. "Bridge," Hakaka said. The old woman clicked her tongue, which meant good.

Before dusk, Sa-la palmed two pebbles from WOOD and set them under FIRE. La-la saw. She didn’t speak. She moved the cup. Sa-la’s knee found it. He caught the cup and the look from Hakaka in the same breath. He put the pebbles back. "Write the work your hands remember," Hakaka said. "Or your knee will."

They read at dusk.

Ya-la read the marks like steps: "Water four. Rope two. Net one new. Wood three. Fire kept, once wrong then right. Fever watched. Honey paid. Gift and debt level." She glanced to Hakaka. "Almost," Hakaka said, tapping the meat-shadow. She added a small ash line for the bite the old woman hadn’t taken. "Truth is the whole mouth."

They sat a little straighter. The ledger did that.

They set TOMORROW with three empty headings: CLAY, LINE, WATCH. Ya-la put a single ghost-mark under CLAY--not a promise, only a wish. She blew it away with two soft puffs. "Wish belongs in the chest," she said. "Not the floor." The old woman hummed, which meant finally.

Hakaka handed Sa-la a fresh twig. "Sweep." He hesitated. "We’ll forget," he said. "No," Hakaka said. "We will carry." She pointed at his palm, at La-la’s shoulders, at the tug on the robe, at Taro’s flat stone, at Ya-la’s shard with the notch. "Many ledgers."

Sa-la swept. The marks rose and disappeared. Only the palm stripe faintly remained, like breath on cold stone. He smudged that too. The floor went back to bare, packed earth. Their backs still knew the count.

Night. WATCH held. FIRE stayed. The cup moved once and nobody hit it.

Before sleep, Ya-la took one black-ended counting stick and burned its other end clean. She set it by the door with the others, black to one side, clean to the other, the way she’d said: hungry/ fed, storm / clear, lost / found. Taro touched the row with two fingers. "How many we didn’t starve," he said. "Today: one more," La-la said.

They lay down. The day had been written and erased, then written again in sore hands, in a shard with a notch, in a bone tuft tugging at a seam. The ledger had no weight, but it held them.

In the grey before morning, the old woman woke and moved the cup back where a knee might find it. Just in case memory got ideas.

Badge #15 -- Field Oven

Mark: clay dome · leaf seal · draw-hole
Vow: Heat once, feed many.

They picked a place out of wind, on hard ground that didn’t drink. Hakaka drew a circle with a stick. "Belly-sized," she said. "Not house-sized."

La-la hauled clay from the bank and stamped it with her heels until it went from slick to willing. Taro pounded dry grass and crushed shell into it for temper. Ya-la carried water; Sa-la carried questions.

"Sand first," Hakaka said. They heaped damp sand into a round back, the way you build a sleeping dog. "Door here." She cut a mouth as wide as her hand and two fingers. "And a small hole at the crown so smoke can tell us its color," the old woman added, settling on a rock.

They laid clay over the sand with hands like paddles--flat, not poking. First a thin skin. Then another. Sa-la smacked a lump high and proud; the clay sagged. "Thin and even," La-la said, smoothing it back down. "Thick breaks."

Cracks showed at the mouth when it started to dry; Ya-la stroked them with wet fingers until they closed. Taro pressed a flat stone at the threshold so the door wouldn’t chew itself.

They left it to stiffen while they cut arm-length sticks. "Small fire first," Hakaka said. "Oven has to learn heat like a child learns walking." Sa-la fed it too fast and the dome hissed. A hairline opened. He flushed, pulled wood back, and breathed until the smoke darkened then cleared. The crack waited, no bigger.

All afternoon they nursed it--stick, ember, wait. The dome changed voice: from damp thud to a clean, hollow knock when La-la tapped it with a twig. "It’s listening," she said.

Evening came cold. They burned the inside bright, then raked the coals out into a shallow trench for tea. The dome held a red glow that was more memory than fire. "Now the roots," Hakaka said.

Ya-la rubbed tubers with a little salt and ash. Taro wrapped them in leaves and damp clay flats as thin as bark. Sa-la slid them in with a forked stick. The old woman pinched the door with a double fold of wet grass and slapped a clay tile against it. "Seal soft," she said. "Hard seals leak."

They sat on their heels and listened to the oven talk--little ticks as the clay settled, a sigh now and then from the door. Sa-la pressed his ear to the dome. "It’s breathing." Hakaka nudged him back. "So are you. Wait."

The smell rose slow, from raw soil to sweet root. When Ya-la said, "There," they pulled the door and rolled the bundles out. The clay shells cracked under thumb; steam lifted. They passed pieces, blew on fingers, burned lips, grinned anyway. The old woman chewed and gave one single nod that meant three things.

They wrote the rule in ash on the ground, where knees had already polished it:

-- Form with sand; skin with thin clay.
-- Temper the clay or it sulks.
-- Small fire teaches; big fire tests.
-- Knock for hollow; watch for hairlines.
-- Rake coals; load fast; seal soft.
-- Wait until the wait turns sweet.
-- Heat once, feed many.

They wiped the line. The rule stayed in their hands.

After, they slid the coals back in for one more bake--flat cakes of pounded seed, the kind you eat with two fingers and a thanks. Hakaka left the door propped with a sliver so smoke would find the crown hole and not their eyes. Sa-la reached to close it; she shook her head. "Let it breathe."

Across the river, the scar-mouthed woman lifted her chin at the new glow. Ya-la held up a flat cake, broke it, set half in the reed pouch, and sent it across. It came back with a small clay plug, shaped neat, just the size of their draw-hole. La-la set it in place and smiled with only one side of her mouth.

Night cooled and the dome held on. They lay near it for the warm and for what it promised: mornings with work that didn’t bite as hard. Sa-la pressed his palm to the clay and learned the feel of enough. The oven forgot nothing. Neither did they.

Badge #14 -- First Weir

Mark: forked stakes · brush fence · basket gate
Vow: Take plenty; leave one path.

They walked until the river ran straight and shallow, the bottom clean as a palm. Hakaka paced it with her heel. "Here," she said. "Push with the river, not against."

La-la cut stakes from willow and split their bottoms. Taro carried them to the bar, two at a time, shoulders set. Sa-la wanted the longest; Hakaka handed him the short ones. "You’ll place more and learn faster," she said. He didn’t argue.

They drove the stakes slanted downstream so the push would seat them. La-la’s stone mallet spoke a steady beat. Ya-la brought brush--flex and tangle--and wove it belly-high between stakes. The fence bent and hummed. Good.

They shaped the fence into a wide V pointing with the current. At the narrow end they set a basket with its mouth upstream and a flat stone to keep it from wandering. "Gate here," Hakaka said, tapping a gap a hand-span wide beside the basket. Sa-la reached to close it. Hakaka caught his wrist. "Leave a lane," she said. "River pays us when we pay it back."

First light brought shadows with fins. The fence nudged them toward the mouth. Some slid past the gate and were gone. Sa-la’s jaw went tight. "Wait," La-la said. "Full fences break; patient ones fill."

The first try failed small: a push of water rolled the basket and it sulked sideways, empty. Taro saw it first. He set a second stone on the far side and twisted the basket’s mouth to face truer. Ya-la tied two reeds across the throat to take the first kick of water, not the basket ribs. The mouth held its shape.

By noon the basket knocked once, soft. Ya-la lifted it with Hakaka. Silver flashed--two fish, fat enough for stew and a gift across. They bled them quick and clean. Sa-la grinned, then checked the gate with his thumb. It was still open. He let his grin live.

They worked the line all day: brush tighter where water lied, higher where it climbed. La-la’s hands were striped red; she rubbed silt on the sting and kept weaving. Taro learned the mallet’s rhythm and matched it without showing off. The old woman sat where wind met shade. "Good ear," she said to the fence. No one asked if she meant the wood or the ones who built it.

Across the water the scar-mouth woman watched from her stones. Hakaka lifted the basket and tipped two fish to the current. One slid the gate and was gone. The other rode the flow to their bar. The cliff-man’s gaze moved, slow as a log in flood. He raised a hand, palm out. Fair.

Near dusk a bigger push came--backs cutting the run like knife tips. The basket thunked harder than before, tried to climb free. Sa-la leaped; La-la’s hand thumped his chest. "Not when it’s loud," she said. They waited a breath. The push eased. They lifted together. Four fish. Enough.

They shared the first two, hung two to smoke, and set bones to boil. Ya-la took a small one across in the reed pouch with a blue strip tied near the loop. The woman on the far bar sent back a coil of thin cord and a nod that held, then let go. They both watched the open gate and did not touch it.

They wrote the rule in the mud where feet would step it clean by morning:

-- Stakes lean with flow.
-- Brush to hum, not wall.
-- Basket mouth true; stone each side.
-- One gate always open.
-- Lift when the knocking speaks soft.
-- Thank with hands, not words.

They wiped the line. The rule stayed anyway.

Night cooled the water. The fence kept its shape and whispered. Sa-la lay on his side and watched the gate. "It bothers me," he said. "Good," Hakaka answered. "It bothers the river too. That keeps us both from sleeping stupid."

He slept. The river did what it does. The gate stayed open. The basket learned the sound of enough.

Badge #13 -- Field Oven

Mark: shallow pit · hot stones · mud lid
Vow: Heat tight; wait sweet.

They chose a place where wind came gentle and the ground rang when you kicked it. Hakaka pressed her heel, then her palm. "Dry," she said. "Good." La-la drew a circle with a stick, big enough for two hands of fish and a basket of roots. Sa-la started digging with a rib scraper. Taro took a flat stone and widened the sides. Ya-la brought leaves--broad, wet, green--and a bundle of thin branches.

The old woman sat on a folded cloak, knees up. "Make the mouth small," she said. "Heat runs when it sees sky." No one argued.

They gathered stones from the slope, not the river. La-la tapped each one with a pebble. Good ones rang. Bad ones thudded or cracked. "No water inside," she said. Sa-la tried to sneak in a smooth, pretty one from the stream. It popped in the small fire when they tested, a sharp report like a breaking tooth. He flinched, then nodded. Lesson held.

They lit a clean fire in the pit and fed it short sticks till the pit glowed. Stones went in on top, one by one with forked sticks. The fire hissed, then settled. "More," Hakaka said. "Stones first, hunger second." They kept feeding until the stones carried their own light.

While the pit heated, Ya-la washed the fish at the door stones from the weir. She ran her thumb along each belly, quick and kind. La-la peeled roots with the bone scraper, shaved them thin. Taro cracked two bulbs and set the cloves aside. The old woman handed him a packet of salt wrapped in leaf. "Little," she said. "Enough to wake, not to drown."

Sa-la cut green branches to make a grill. "Cross," La-la said, guiding his hands. "Let steam climb." He nodded, tongue at his teeth, and made a good grid.

When the stones glowed dull red, Hakaka raked the coals out to the side with a forked stick and a flat board. The pit breath was a hot fist. She waited a count till the air stopped biting. "Fast burns," she said. "We want slow."

They lined the pit with wet leaves. Steam puffed. Sa-la laughed at the sound and then kept his face out of it. Fish went down first, bellies stuffed with sliced root and the small cloves. More leaves. Thin cuts of meat from the last of the deer. More leaves. Taro poured a handful of water around the edge. It hissed and smelled like clean earth. "For breath," he said, and no one told him he was showing off.

They laid the green grill over the leaves and set a mud lid on top--clay mixed quick with straw and scraped with a rib to make it thin and strong. La-la sealed the edges with the heel of her hand. "Tight," she said. "Let the heat do the walking, not the wind."

"Vent," the old woman said, tapping the lid. Hakaka poked one small hole with a stick. A thin thread of steam rose and wandered. "Good," she said. "Draw."

Sa-la reached for the lid after the first finger of smell found him. Hakaka caught his wrist. "You want hot smoke and no dinner," she said. He pulled back and stood with his hands on the back of his neck. He faced away, breathing the good hurt.

They waited without talking. Wind shifted. The vent steamed steady, then thinner. La-la crouched and put her ear to the lid. "It talks softer," she said. The old woman nodded. "When the pot stops complaining," she said, "the food has learned its lesson."

Taro fidgeted. Ya-la handed him a stick. "Mark the wait," she said. He scratched short lines in the dirt by the pit, one for each count of a slow song. When he had ten, she put her palm over his. "Another five," she said. He exhaled like a pipe going cold and drew them.

Rain tried and failed. The sky had nothing serious in it. Sa-la checked the vent again and again and then made himself step back three paces so he wouldn’t touch what would hurt him. La-la stood where she could stop him if he forgot.

When the steam thinned to nothing and the lid smelled like clean clay, Hakaka lifted one edge with a forked stick. No blast. Good sign. She pried and set the lid aside on two stones. Steam rose lazy. The leaves nearest the lid were dark and sweet. Taro closed his eyes and smiled without showing teeth. Sa-la grinned full and didn’t hide it.

They pulled leaves away with sticks, not fingers. "Hot remembers," Ya-la said. Fish came out whole, flesh sliding from bones with no argument. Meat had the shine that means done. The roots were soft and carried the smell of the cloves. La-la tested one slice between thumb and forefinger. It yielded and held. She nodded once, pleased small.

They set food on clean bark and gave the first piece to the old woman. She chewed slow, then faster. "Teeth can sit down," she said. "Good work." Sa-la laughed and almost clapped, then didn’t. Hakaka gave him a look that said you are seen.

They ate without hurry. No one spoke big words. Grease ran down Sa-la’s wrist and he licked it, then wiped the rest on his forearm and grinned at the streak. Ya-la handed him a damp scrap to finish the job. "Don’t feed flies," she said. He nodded and used it.

Across the river the scar-mouth woman lifted her head like a fox catching scent. She watched the smoke shape. The cliff-man raised a hand, palm out, as if to say good work or we smell you or both. Hakaka lifted the vent stick in answer and turned it in a circle. The woman nodded once and went back to her own fire.

When they were full enough, they packed the bones tight and set them to boil in a small pot to make tomorrow honest. "Fat first," the old woman reminded. Ya-la skimmed a little for her cup. "Quiet throat," she said, and the old woman pretended not to smile.

Sa-la wanted to dig the pit out and build it better right then. La-la put a hand on his shoulder. "Hot rocks crack when you hurry them," she said. "Let them sleep." He scuffed his heel and then sat with his palms on his knees, eyes finding the vent hole again like it owed him something. "It will be there," Hakaka said. "If not, we’ll make it again. Easier next time."

They let the lid dry by the small fire, turning it once so it wouldn’t warp. Taro chipped a notch in the rim to seat the vent stick better. Ya-la scratched a small mark on the lid’s top--a circle with one short line--so anyone would know where to lift and where to leave alone.

When the pot of bones was set on the coals, they wrote the rules in ash and said them through, each to hold one:

-- Stones from slope, not river.
-- Heat the pit till stones carry light.
-- Leaves wet, layers thin.
-- Lid thin; edges sealed; one vent.
-- Wait past the first good smell.
-- Sticks for lifting; fingers last.
-- First piece to the one who needs soft.
-- Bones to tomorrow.

They wiped the marks. The rules stayed anyway.

Night brought a thin chill. The old woman’s cough was small. Sa-la lay on his back and watched sparks drift up like insects that knew where to go. "We made a small earth with weather," he said, quiet. La-la grunted. "We kept it from lying," she said.

Before sleep, Hakaka used the last heat of the pit to dry two new cords and warm the damp in the robe where seams had argued with the day. She pressed her palm to the lid and left no mark. "Warm holds longer when it has a home," she said.

In the morning, the stones would still be warm enough to start the day’s fire without smoke in the eyes. They would not have to beg the neighbors for a coal. That was a kind of wealth.

They slept with good bellies and clean hands. The pit kept a low promise under the dirt.

Badge #12 -- First Weir

Mark: V of stakes · brush fence · basket throat
Vow: Take only what returns; leave a door.

Morning gave them a cold river with a slow shoulder. Hakaka walked the bank with her hands behind her back, counting without numbers. "Here," she said. "Bottom is firm. Current is honest." La-la squatted and pushed a stake down with both palms. It bit and stood. "Good ground."

Taro carried a bundle of cut willow, ends clean and sharp. Sa-la dragged brush thatched with last year’s leaves. Ya-la brought twine and a basket they had woven for this. The old woman sat on a rock with the sun on her back. She did not talk much when there was work that needed breath.

Across the water the scar-mouth woman stood with her boy. The cliff of a man came later with stakes over his shoulder like spare ribs. He looked at Hakaka, then at the open river. He set one stake in the shallows on his side, nothing more. A question in wood.

Hakaka stepped into the cold up to the knee and gasped through her teeth. "Edges," she said. "We give the river an easier place to go." La-la nodded. They set the first line: stakes in a row, slanting downstream, a hand apart. Sa-la hammered with a round stone, eager, missing once and bruising knuckles. He hissed, then kept going. "Hit your own hand last," La-la said without looking.

They bent the line into a long V that pointed into the flow. The open end faced upstream. Brush went between stakes, butt to the current, thin at first so water could learn the path. "Not a wall," Ya-la said. "A guide."

Across the way, the woman mirrored them. The cliff-man set his stakes slow, straight, sure. The boy watched both banks at once. When the two Vs reached toward each other like careful hands, they left a gap where the points nearly met. Hakaka lifted a flat stone and set it there in the shallows. "Door," she said. "We take less to take longer."

Taro frowned. "We need fish now." Hakaka didn’t argue. She touched his wrist, two beats. "Now and later." He looked away, then nodded once, not happy and not fighting.

They tied the basket to the narrow throat of the V on their side, mouth toward the gap. Ya-la checked the lashings twice, then a third time as if her fingers could smell their weakness. "If it pulls free," she said, "we feed the river a day’s work." Sa-la tied another line from the basket to a root on shore. He tested by leaning his weight and letting it take him a little. The line held. He grinned without showing teeth.

They fed brush into the fence until the river’s voice changed--a lower, steady talk. Drift that would have swung past now nosed the slant and slid along it like a tired animal choosing a path. "Let it tire," La-la said. "Then ask."

The first fish came accidental--a silver back bumping the brush and flicking through the gap into the basket like it had decided that way itself. The basket shook once and stilled. Sa-la whooped. Hakaka lifted a hand and he made the sound smaller and kept it in his chest.

Ya-la pulled the basket in slow. She didn’t lift it. She turned it toward her knee and let the water drain, then raised it enough to see. Two fish, one small, one big as a forearm. She gave the small one to the river through the door. The big one she knocked clean with a flat stone. "One to teach the others," the old woman said. No one argued.

The cliff-man watched that and did the same on his side. He took two, let one go. The scar-mouth woman looked at Hakaka and gave a small nod that said we understand the same thing.

They worked the fence in short bursts. Brush wants to float; they pinned it with thin cross-branches woven through like stitches. Sa-la wanted to stuff the gaps tight. La-la pulled some out with two sharp tugs. "Fast flood, fence goes. Loose flood, fence lives." She left places that would tear away clean instead of dragging the whole weir to the sea.

Midday, the current strengthened. A log came down like a bad idea, rolling slow with a hidden speed. Hakaka spotted it and pointed. "Hands off." They stepped back. The log struck the slant, pressed, then the loose ties broke as they were meant to, giving it a straight line out and away. The fence looked injured but not ruined. Sa-la swore soft and honest. "We built the break in," Ya-la said. "Or the river builds it for us."

They repaired what tore. Taro split new willow with a bone wedge and fed La-la lengths with clean, white insides. He worked with his mouth shut, which was new. The old woman slept a little with her chin on her chest and woke to say, "Leave a stone marker where the door is. Night remembers poorly." Sa-la set three flat stones in a line, small to big, just upstream of the gap. He set them twice, because the first time they looked clever and not clear.

By late light, fish gathered like thoughts. They came along the slant, turned at the V, and tried the door. Some learned it and slid away. Some made the basket shake and stay. Ya-la pulled at a steady pace, not greedy. "We take what we can clean today," she said. "No rot."

Across the river the boy copied Sa-la’s stone marks. He got the pattern wrong the first time, big to small. Sa-la laughed, then stopped and made the motion with his hands--small to big like a road into a narrow place. The boy fixed it and raised his chin, pleased. The scar-mouth woman pretended not to see either of them.

The cliff-man brought over a length of rope made of something darker than reed. He held it out across the gap on a long pole. Hakaka pushed their basket line toward him with a branch. Lines touched, tangled, then found a way to coil around each other where the current wouldn’t worry them. The two weirs leaned toward being one without stepping into each other’s soil. "With," Ya-la said under her breath, and the word felt right.

They ate while they worked--thin strips cut quick and swallowed without ceremony. The old woman chewed slow and then handed the last piece to Sa-la. He started to hand it back. She pushed his fingers closed. "Grow," she said. He did.

At dusk, they opened the door wider and left the basket empty. "Night is for teaching," Hakaka said. "Day is for taking." Sa-la didn’t like it, but he set the stones so the gap was clear even in dim light. Taro stood with his hands in the river and felt the pull and the let-go. He closed his eyes like he was fixing the shape inside him.

They wrote the rules in ash and wiped them flat when the mouths had said them true: set stakes slanting downstream; weave loose enough to tear clean; leave a door and mark it; basket at the throat, not the mouth; take what you can use fresh; let some go always; fix what the river teaches you to fix; no work alone in fast water.

Rain came thin and cold. They covered the fish with wet leaves while they cleaned, guts for the dogs far from camp, skins hung high. Sa-la slipped once on the slick rock, caught himself with both hands, and laughed at the sound he made. La-la bumped his shoulder. "Feet think better when fed," she said, and shoved a piece into his mouth.

They slept to the sound of water practicing its lesson against their work.

At first light the door stones were still where he’d left them. Sa-la lifted the basket and found it full enough to make his shoulders speak. He braced his feet and did not ask for help. Hakaka watched and didn’t step in until he needed it, which he didn’t, and that became a fact he would carry.

Across the river, the scar-mouth woman lifted her basket at the same time and set it down in mirror. She held up two fingers, then touched her breastbone and pointed to the gap. Two left, mine. Hakaka held up three, touched the basket, and pointed to the door. Three left, ours. They both nodded. A law without a voice, agreed anyway.

Before they closed for the day, Hakaka picked the smallest fish from their catch, still bright. She bent to the door and let it slip through her fingers into the slow seam. It flashed and was gone. Sa-la looked at her hand. "Why that one?" "So the river tells that story too," she said.

The old woman stood with help and shuffled to the bank. She looked down at the V and the door and the neat stones. "You made a mouth," she said. "Teach it to speak kind and it will feed long."

They banked brush at the weak places, left the door clear, and tied the basket high. The cliff-man put his palm to his chest and then to the river and then to the weir like he was stamping all three together. Ya-la did the same, and they both smiled, small and tired and not at each other.

When they walked home, Sa-la kept looking over his shoulder at the stones marking the door. "Will they stay?" he asked. "They’ll move," La-la said. "So will we." He nodded. He would come at dawn and set them right again. He already knew the right places like he knew his own hands.

That night they ate fish cooked on green sticks and laid on hot stones, and no one said there had been a better meal. The weir talked in the dark, low and steady. In the morning, it would talk again, and they would listen, and leave the door.

Badge #11 -- Field Oven

Mark: clay ring · draw-hole · cap
Vow: Heat likes tight; wait until wait turns sweet.

They chose a flat shoulder of ground out of the wind and not far from water. Hakaka knelt and pressed her palm to the dirt. "Hard enough," she said. "Not so hard the clay won’t bite." La-la nodded. "We build where feet won’t trip." She scratched a circle with a stick, big as her arms could make.

Taro brought shovel stones--the flat ones that cut earth if you ask right. Sa-la carried damp clay in a woven sling that leaked on his leg and made him look like he’d walked through a low river. Ya-la had sand in a basket and a small bag of salt. "A pinch keeps cracks from running," she said. The old woman watched with her lips thin, saving her breath. "Don’t make it pretty," she warned. "Make it strong."

They mixed clay with sand and a little salt under shade. La-la trod the heap with both feet until it answered her heel with a deep, close sound. Sa-la wanted to add more water. Ya-la stopped his hand. "Thirsty clay lies," she said. "It will drink and then sag." He left it and learned the patience in his neck.

They rolled the first rope of clay and laid it along the scratch, pressing it flat, then another coil on top, staggering the joins. The ring grew up from the ground like a slow snake sitting down. Taro smoothed the inner wall with a wet stone until it felt like a bowl. La-la left a narrow mouth on one side, low to the ground. "Feed here," she said. "Work from here. Smoke wants out the other way."

Hakaka marked a small, round hole opposite the mouth, a hand above the ground. "Draw," she said. She pushed a smooth stick through and left it there so the hole would keep its circle. "When breath goes in, fire gives back." The old woman almost smiled. "Teach it to breathe. Good."

They made a cap: a shallow dome the size of a big platter, with a rim that would sit over the ring’s mouth. Ya-la pressed a loop of reed into the cap’s top so you could lift it with a stick. Sa-la wanted to make a little bird on the top. La-la knocked the thought out of the clay with her thumb. "Later." He flushed, then laughed at himself and smoothed the smear. "Later," he said.

They let the ring rest in shade while they gathered fuel: finger-width sticks for heat, arm-thick for bank, chips for talk. Taro found a dead root heavy as a boy and cut it into rounds. Hakaka shook her head. "That is for overnight, not now." Taro lifted one piece anyway. "I like to know it’s waiting." Hakaka said nothing. She liked that too.

At dusk they fired the ring. Small bundle first, a thin fire that talked fast and light. Ya-la fed it like a hungry dog you don’t trust yet--often, little. The dampness came out of the clay in a fine sweat. Hairline lines showed, then closed again. "Don’t smile at it," La-la said to Sa-la when he grinned at the first lick of flame through the draw-hole. "It will remember your teeth and crack there." He shut his mouth and watched.

The dome cap dried near the heat, turning from dark to the color of baked bread. Sa-la tapped it with a stick. It answered low and true. "Not yet," Hakaka said. "It hasn’t learned how to hold." They let the ring eat for a long time, always feeding from the mouth, never starving it and never letting it rage. When the inner wall glowed a little like a warm stone at noon, Hakaka nodded. "Enough. We teach it food."

They raked coals to the back wall and swept the floor with a green branch. Ya-la slid a flat stone inside to warm. She had mixed ground seed and water and a pinch of salt into a small paste, smooth as she could. La-la spread a thin round with wet fingers on a leaf, then tipped it onto the hot stone. The paste stuck and sizzled like a good promise.

They set the cap over the mouth, leaving a finger’s gap at the bottom. Smoke gathered, looked for any foolish place to go, then learned the road of the draw-hole. The little plume lifted true. The old woman put her hand just inside the gap and winced, pleased. "Heat likes tight," she said. "You did not forget."

Sa-la put his nose near the gap. Hakaka flicked his ear without looking. He moved back a hand. "I was just smelling." "Smell later," La-la said. "Wait now." He waited, but his foot told the dirt a story.

Ya-la counted slow breaths. After forty, she lifted the cap a finger. Steam kissed her knuckles and did not bite. "Not yet," she said. After a while more, she lifted again. The round had gone from wet to dull to a small, honest color. The edge lifted with a whisper. La-la slid a stick under and turned it. It let go. "Now," La-la said, not with her mouth.

They ate the first flat with their fingers while it still complained about leaving the stone. It tasted of smoke and seed and something like the inside of a new bowl. Sa-la burned his tongue and did not spit; he chased the hurt with breath and pride. The old woman chewed slow, eyes wet but not from feeling. "It will keep," she said. "Make three more before sleep. Your morning selves will call you smart."

They learned the oven’s temper the next day. Too much wood too fast and the paste blistered and sulked. Too little and it lay asleep in the middle. "A good mouth and a good back," La-la said, tapping, listening. "We feed it like a child, and like a child it will tell us what sort of day it’s having."

They cooked fish at the mouth: the thin ones laid on green sticks across, the thicker bellies nested in leaves tucked near the cap. The skin tightened and loosened and told Ya-la when to turn. The smell made the dogs forget their manners; they were reminded.

The clay cracked once, a thin line from mouth toward the side. Sa-la saw it first and felt it with a finger he didn’t deserve. The line widened. "Sorry," he said out loud to no one and everyone. Ya-la pressed a paste of clay and ash into the cut while the wall was still warm. She spoke to the oven like she would to a baby that had worked too hard. "Hold, hold." It held. She left a thumbprint where the fix sat. "Now it remembers you," La-la said. "Don’t make it flattering."

In the afternoon the wind turned on them. Smoke sulked at the mouth and tried to crawl into eyes. Hakaka moved a basket and the old woman’s seat two steps to the left. "Wind door," she said. She knocked a reed screen together and set it so the mouth had a leeward ear. The change was small and the result was large; smoke learned manners again. "Door wants a leeward ear," La-la said, liking how it sounded because it was true.

The cliff of a man came at dusk with a bundle of split wood cut to wrist length. He set it down without stepping inside the circle of work. Hakaka walked the split pile with her eyes and then her hand, measuring without measuring. "That’s right," she said--two words he could carry. The scar-mouth woman held up a palm full of something the color of dusk. "River clay," Ya-la guessed. The woman nodded, tapped the crack-line on their oven, then her own hip, then the bundle. Shared problems; shared ways to fix them. They traded a flat round for a pinch of the smooth dark, wrapped in leaf.

Night came with a thin, hard cold. They banked the oven with a back wall of coals pulled in tight and the cap left breathing small. La-la dragged the biggest root round near. "Not inside," Hakaka said. "By the mouth. Let the oven remember heat but not choke on it." La-la obeyed and sat next to the warm clay like it was a small animal leaning against her for trust. Sa-la fell asleep with his head near the draw-hole, which ticked and sighed like a patient chest.

At dawn the cap still held a little hand of warmth. Ya-la blew through the hole and fed a handful of dry grass. A low glow answered and climbed the grass like a careful friend. "It kept," she said, and even the old woman made a sound you could count as pleased.

They made rules and wrote them in wet ash, then wiped them flat when all had said them true: build with sand in the clay; leave a draw-hole and keep it round; feed from the mouth, breathe through the back; cap down for keeping, lifted for turning; wait until the wait turns sweet; never leave it hot and alone; fix cracks when warm; don’t teach it your teeth.

On the third day Sa-la tried meat inside because patience had looked away. He thrust a strip too deep and too fast; fat spat and took to flame. Smoke turned harsh and the oven coughed. Hakaka grabbed his wrist and pulled his hand out before he cooked it. La-la closed the cap tight, then lifted it an inch and left the draw-hole wide. The harsh went out like a bad thought told no audience. Sa-la stood with his hand in cold clay at the river’s lip, eyes angry with himself. Ya-la came and smeared honey on the reddened skin; the bees had offered, and now the sweetness had a second job. "So it minds its manners," she said. He nodded, small. "I’ll mind mine." The old woman snorted. "News."

They found the oven could dry reeds, warm a sleeping stone, crisp the fat on a fish skin so it broke like good laughter. It could take wet from shoes, save coals for mornings, and give back the taste of yesterday’s smoke to today’s food so memory had a mouth-feel. "Story fire," Ya-la said once, very quiet, and no one called it a name again, but the name stayed anyway.

On a day of thin rain, steam rolled off the cap like breath in winter. Taro rubbed his hands, put the backs of his cold fingers against the clay rim, and closed his eyes as if listening. "I like a thing that holds," he said. "Even when the sky forgets us." Hakaka didn’t say she felt the same. She just fed the mouth with a length of split wood the cliff of a man had cut and set the cap neat, twice.

Before sleep, La-la ran her knuckles around the ring and found no loose talk in the wall. At the draw-hole she paused and didn’t poke a stick through. She looked and left it. "It breathes right," she said to the dirt, then lay down and rolled her shoulder until the day clicked out. The oven ticked to itself, keeping one small ember the color of a patient eye.

In the morning, flats warmed fast, fish took smoke proper, and no one burned a tongue. The old woman ate slow and watched the little plume at the draw-hole go steady. "I have cooked on worse mouths," she said. "Plenty worse." Hakaka touched the clay and felt it hold. "We can keep this," she said. "If we remember why we keep it tight--and when to open." Sa-la nodded like the rule had a place to live in him now. He stayed long enough to finish his flat, then ran errands without being told.

By evening the oven had become part of the ground the way a path becomes more path the more you use it. It was ugly and perfect. It remembered hands. It warmed what asked. It kept a little of yesterday for tomorrow, and didn’t brag.

Badge #10 -- Stone Weir

Mark: V of stones · reed gate · spill-way
Vow: Let the river choose the point; leave a door.

They walked the shallows at first light. Hakaka stepped heel then toe to feel the floor. La-la watched the way the water leaned. Taro tested with a pole and nodded when the tip kissed gravel, not silt. "Here," he said. "Bottom rises. Banks come close. Fish push hard past this place."

Across the water the scar-mouth woman stood with the boy and the cliff of a man. She raised a hand. Hakaka raised one back and pointed at the narrowing. The woman looked left, looked right, and tapped the same place with her foot. Good.

They scratched a V on each side with sticks, point downstream. "Mouth here," La-la said. "Wings there and there." She set the first stone and listened with her palm. "Flat on flat. No wobble."

They carried stones, not the biggest--honest ones that could be held. The cliff of a man took larger, set them like he’d been waiting to do it. Sa-la tried to stack fast. La-la knocked one down with two fingers. "No fast if it falls," she said. "We stack low and wide. River will try to move what is proud."

Ya-la cut reeds where they grew thick and green. The boy cut too, keeping his blue scrap out of the water. They wove a square--tight at first, then tighter when Sa-la tested it and the weave gaped. "Gate," Ya-la said. She tied two cross sticks at the top for a carry hand. The woman showed a knot Sa-la didn’t know. He learned it with his fingers, twice, until it stayed without a thought.

By noon the wings reached most of the way from each bank. The point of the V waited empty. "Spill-way there," Hakaka said, tapping a low run where big water could pass without tearing the work. "We like to live after rain."

They pressed mud between stones where the water tried to talk through. "Press and feel," La-la said. "When your thumb doesn’t find a mouth, move on." Taro packed gravel at the feet of the wings so the current would push on ground, not under. The cliff of a man pressed his weight along the V and watched the water change tone. He grunted once. Approval.

They set the reed gate at the point, tied to two stakes. It hung in the flow and pushed back true. Sa-la smiled. The gate pushed his smile flat and then let it up again.

"Rule," Hakaka said, and spoke it to both banks. "We leave a door." She lifted two stones from the very end and set them aside. A gap the width of her hand shone. "Small ones pass. Breeders pass. We eat long, not just loud."

The old woman had come as far as the bank allowed and sat on a dry log. "You build a hand to catch water," she said. "You will learn what it slaps."

They tried at dusk when fish nose hardest. Ya-la set a string of pebbles on a flat rock--three for first check, five for second, seven for last. "We go when the shadow reaches each stone," she said. Sa-la wanted to bend the plan. Hakaka looked once. He didn’t.

At the first check the gate held. A single small fish nosed the weave and slid through the gap like a thought. "Good," La-la said. "Door works." Taro lifted the gate by its cross sticks. The wings coughed two finger-length fish into his basket. He dropped them back and said nothing. Ya-la saw and tucked that away.

Second check: the water had found a soft seam under the left wing. It whispered there and took a handful of mud. "It always will," La-la said. They packed it tight with gravel and heel, then set a stone that fit the hole like it had been waiting to be used. The cliff of a man watched the fix and mirrored it on his side. No talk.

At the last check the river had brought more. The gate quivered with life. Sa-la lifted too fast and the square bucked. Two fish leapt and were gone. He flushed. La-la put her palm on the cross sticks with his. "Together. Up slow. Let the heads find stillness." They lifted, steady. Taro slid the flat basket under and pulled the gate over it. The fish slapped the wicker and stayed.

They counted out loud so no boast could change the number. Ten fish. La-la pointed to three with swollen bellies and let them go. Hakaka pointed to two thin ones and let them go too. "Keep seven," she said. "We eat now and not hate ourselves later."

Across the water the woman counted as well and made her own cuts. She held one fish up until Hakaka held one too, then let it go. They laughed once, not loud.

They hung the catch from a forked pole and walked back with the gate under Sa-la’s arm. The boy across did the same. The cliff of a man came last, hands empty, eyes on the way the water pulled at the outer stones. He moved one with his foot and stood longer to see if the line held. It did.

At the fire they cleaned fish fast. Ya-la rubbed a smear of salt inside each belly, slid a strip of green along the spine, and set them by the oven’s mouth for smoke and heat. The smell made the old woman’s face soften before her words did. "About time," she said, which for her meant well done.

They ate and left bones for the dogs well away from sleeping. Sa-la reached for a crisp tail. Hakaka shook her head once. He put it with the others high out of reach. The lesson was old. It held.

Next day the river rose a thumb. The spill-way took the push and saved the wings. In the afternoon a heavy branch came down and struck the right side hard. It shifted the outside three stones. Taro saw it first and shouted. Sa-la ran and braced the gap with his body without thinking and took the hit in his thigh. La-la and the cliff of a man set stones back while the branch jammed against Sa-la and used him as a post. When the line was true again, Hakaka pushed the branch off with a pole and caught Sa-la by his belt before the river took him. He stood up slow and did not talk for a while. Later he admitted the river had bitten him purple. The old woman gave him a look that said good and stupid at once. He took both.

Toward evening they found a handful of small ones trapped in a little pocket behind a wobbly stone. Ya-la put them back. She set her palm over the pocket and felt the pull. "This is a bad mouth," she said. "It will eat small fish for no reason." They filled it with gravel until it went quiet.

They made more rules and kept them short: check three times then leave it; lift the gate with two hands; count in the open; let small and heavy-bellied go; stand out of the spill when rain thinks loud; fix what leaks before you sleep. Hakaka pressed each with a stick into wet sand, then smeared the sand flat when all had said them. Taro never looked away while she did it.

On the third day the cliff of a man walked to the water with a small bundle. He set a stone where their line was weakest and tapped it twice, then set the bundle on the rock and stepped back. Ya-la went to the edge and used the reed line to drag it across. In it: three short stakes, sharpened right, and a coil of bark cord you could trust. Hakaka put a small pouch of salt in the bundle and sent it back. No one clapped.

That night the weir held a soft song. Sa-la lay on his back and listened. "We made a place the river speaks to," he said.

"We made a place the river agrees with," Hakaka said. "That’s different."

He nodded like he felt the truth of it in his bones.

Before sleep La-la ran her knuckles along the stones one by one. She found a loose one and worked it tight without calling anyone. When she came to the gap at the point she didn’t touch it. She just looked and left it. "One goes on purpose," she said to herself, and liked the shape of that in her mouth.

In the morning the wings were still where hands had put them. The gate lifted without fuss. Five fish, kept honest by what they’d let pass. The old woman ate slow and looked at the river. "I have seen worse laws," she said. "I’ve seen plenty worse."

Hakaka laid her palm on the wet stone and felt it hold. "This one we can keep," she said. "If we remember why we leave the door."

Badge #09 -- Field Oven

Mark: stone oval · clay dome · draw-hole
Vow: Heat likes tight; wait until the wait turns sweet.

They chose a place out of the wind where the ground held firm. Hakaka tapped her heel and listened. "Here," she said. "Dry under the skin." La-la scratched an oval in the dust. Taro carried stones, flat and heavy, and set them edge to edge until the line was a ring.

Ya-la brought clay from the bed in a wet cloth. Sa-la shredded dry grass and mixed it in. The clay took the chaff and held better. "Rib first," La-la said. She bent three green sticks and planted their ends inside the ring, crossing them to make a low spine.

They smeared clay over the ribs a handful at a time. The dome rose slow and honest. Sa-la tried to make it smooth. La-la pressed his hand away. "No pretty," she said. "Tight." She pinched the seams shut with a wet thumb. Hakaka left a mouth just big enough for her forearm and, above it, pressed a small hole for the smoke to pull. "Mouth low, draw high," she said. "Fire likes a path."

The old woman sat on a flat rock and watched the clay darken as the sun took water out of it. "If it falls," she said, "duck."

When the dome held its own weight, they lit a small fire inside--thin sticks, then thicker. The clay steamed and sweated. A line cracked at the crown. Sa-la hissed. Ya-la was already there with a slurry and a scrap of wet cloth. She rubbed the crack until it closed. "It will crack again," she said. "We will close it again."

They fed the fire until the dome turned the color of dry bread. La-la held her palm near the mouth and counted. "Too hot to count to two," she said. "Good." They let the flame settle and pushed char aside. The inside glowed a quiet red.

Taro wanted to work fast. He cut tubers into fists and wrapped them in clay leaves, a trick they’d learned by mistake. Sa-la cleaned a fish the river owed them and laid it on wetted leaves with a reed of salt rubbed along the belly. Ya-la brought a flat pan of damp earth and pressed thin cakes from pounded seeds. Hakaka pinched a little hole in each--"so it breathes and doesn’t blister wrong."

They set a bed of wet greens on the floor, slid the food in with the end of a flat stick, and capped the mouth with a stone they’d saved for this. La-la packed clay around the stone so no breath leaked. Only the draw-hole smoked, thin and steady.

Taro crouched and put his ear to the dome. "I hear it talking," he said.

"It’s steam," Ya-la said. "It stops when the inside turns right."

"How long?" Sa-la asked, because he was hungry.

"Until the wait turns sweet," Hakaka said. She set a stick in the sand near the mouth, leaned to the same angle as the sun. "When the shadow touches the stone, we look."

The old woman coughed and laughed at once. "You made a pot you cannot pick up," she said. "About time."

While they waited, La-la shaped a second stone to fit the mouth better. "The first one is a little loose," she said. Taro carved a notch on the stone’s edge so a hook could pull it without burning fingers. Ya-la ground seeds with a small pestle until the smell changed from bitter to nut. Sa-la drifted toward the mouth twice. Hakaka looked once and he drifted back.

The shadow touched the stone. Hakaka lifted the cap with a hooked stick. Steam pushed her hand. She waited for it to pass, then reached in with a wrapped forearm and tugged the first clay parcel out. The clay skin was hard. She cracked it and peeled it away. The tuber inside was soft and bright. "Done," she said.

The fish came next. The leaves had stuck a little to the floor. La-la slid the flat stick under and lifted slow so the meat stayed whole. The spine came out in one pull. Sa-la made a sound and then swallowed it, remembering. The seed cakes were last--brown at the edge and dry in the middle. Ya-la pressed one and nodded. "Tomorrow a breath of fat," she said. "Today is fine."

They ate there on their heels while the dome breathed. The old woman bit the tuber, closed her eyes, and let her jaw rest between chews. "This is work I respect," she said. "It does not hurry and it pays."

When the food was gone, Hakaka ran a palm over the dome and listened for hollow or soft. She found a thin place near the draw-hole. "Patch," she said. Ya-la brought more slip; Sa-la smoothed it with the back of his fingers. Taro packed small stones around the base on the windward side. "When wind comes with a bad mood, it pushes," he said. "Let it push on the ground, not the wall."

La-la made a rule and said it out loud. "Feed small, seal fast. No peeking until the shadow says." She looked at Sa-la. He nodded. "I know."

Night came with a steady breath. They banked a handful of coals inside the dome and capped the mouth loose. "For morning," Hakaka said. "The oven can keep fire asleep."

It rained a thin rain before dawn. A seam let a drip through; the coals died early. Sa-la found it first and felt the shame rise. He put a hat of clay over the seam before anyone could speak. When Ya-la came, she knelt and touched the cold floor. "We re-light," she said, and did not look at him long. Later she handed him a bone strap with a notch at one end. "For lifting the cap," she said. "Save your skin."

They lit again and fed small. This time the dome held. The draw-hole pulled true; the smoke stood like a rope.

Midday, they tried bread again with a smear of fat. La-la sealed the cap better with the new stone, packed clay tight, and pressed a thumb print on the seam so she could tell if it had shifted. "If the print is broken, someone looked," she said, not looking at Sa-la. He glanced and then stared at the ground. "I won’t," he said.

They waited. The old woman dozed, head on her knees. Taro counted heartbeats, gave up, and counted breaths. Ya-la whittled a small spoon from driftwood. Hakaka watched the shadow creep and did nothing else.

The shadow touched the stone. La-la checked the thumb print--whole. Hakaka lifted the cap. Heat rolled out with a clean smell. The cakes had taken the fat and browned right. Ya-la smiled into her hands and did not show her teeth.

"Name it," the old woman said, mouth already full.

"Keeper," Hakaka said, simple.

Taro nodded as if he had thought of it first. Sa-la ran a finger along the inside wall and showed the gray dust it left. "Tomorrow we smooth more," he said. "No grit in the bread."

"Tomorrow we make two," La-la said. "Small one for quick, big one for feast."

"Feast?" Sa-la said, hopeful.

"When the river pays heavy," Hakaka said. "When our feet deserve meat without apology."

That afternoon, they taught the children of the other bank with their hands. Ya-la pointed to mouth, then to draw-hole, then made the motion of air moving over her palm. The scar-mouth woman nodded and tapped the cap twice to show she understood seal. The boy showed a pinch and a smooth--patch and polish. The cliff of a man watched the way Hakaka checked the wall with her knuckles. He did the same to the one they were building across the water. His face stayed still, but his hands changed.

Before sleep, Hakaka wrote the rules in ash where they cooked: feed small; seal fast; no peeking until shadow says; patch thin places; keep a cap for hand and a cap for heat. Sa-la added one under them. "Thank the thing that holds," he scratched with a stick, and then rubbed it away himself. "In the hands," he said. "We say thanks in the hands."

That night the oven kept the coals alive the way a good chest keeps a cough down. Morning came easy. The old woman sat with her bowl and looked at the dome. "If I had this when I was young," she said, "I would have been kinder for it."

Hakaka laid her palm on the warm clay and felt how it answered. "Keep giving and it keeps giving back," she said. "Like all good work."

They ate and did not talk much. The heat stayed where it should. The day’s hunger felt simple for once. The new thing was not a trick anymore; it was a way.

Badge #08 -- First Path

Mark: grass braid · notch stick · ash arrow
Vow: Leave signs that feed the next feet.

Morning showed three places they needed and one they feared: water, clay, salt edge, and the low ground that tried to drink ankles. They were tired of learning the hard way.

Hakaka drew a short line in ash, then forked it. "We will stop guessing," she said. "The ground can talk. We will teach it words."

La-la cut a handful of reeds and twisted thin braids. "For night," she said, tying one low on a bush at knee height. "Hands read better than eyes when the dark is thick."

Taro trimmed a green stick flat on one side and cut one notch near the tip. "Water," he said, pointing the cut toward the river. He cut two notches lower down for clay, turned them to point along the bank. He set the stick shoulder-high, firm.

"Same height," Ya-la said. "Or we lose the sentence." She tapped the air. "Shoulder for seeing. Knee for feeling."

Sa-la wanted the work that moved. He ran ahead with a spare knife. At the first bend he carved three deep slashes in a soft-barked tree. "Fast," he said, pleased.

La-la looked at the wound and said nothing. She took his wrist and turned his hand over. "It bleeds too much. Say less." She shaved the slashes down until they were two thin, clean cuts. "Two is clay. Keep the mouth small."

They walked the first path slow. From shelter to river: one-notch sticks with the flat faces turned like noses pointing the way. At each fork, Taro laid three stones: two near, one far, the far one on the right side of the right path. "If rain steals the sticks, stones keep the memory."

At the low ground, the earth shivered under foot. Ya-la stood with her weight on her heels and spoke without looking up. "It has the deep sound." La-la drove a thin reed through the crust and it sank almost to her palm. "We go around," she said, and tied a long grass braid across the wrong way so a calf would feel it before the mud did. "Night reads this."

They set a rule there: never mark only the good way. Mark the bad way with a knot you can feel.

On the return they taught the ground a word for danger. Hakaka mixed ash and spit and drew a short arrow on a flat stone, then laid it where feet would notice. "Cross means ‘no,’ pointing means ‘go,’" she said. Ya-la nodded. "No pretty. Just plain."

Afternoon, they walked to the clay bed. Two notches, always. Sa-la kept carving them too deep until his knife hit soft, wet wood and the blade stuck. He yanked and the knife slid and bit his thumb. He swallowed the noise, sucked the blood, and held out the knife. "I’ll cut shallow."

Ya-la tore a strip from the edge of her sleeve and tied his thumb, small and steady. "Say it with me."

"Two is clay," Sa-la said. "At shoulder for eyes. At knee for fingers."

"Good," she said. "Now make ten."

He made eight right and two wrong. La-la turned the wrong ones with a quick flick so the flat faces pointed true. "The ground listens to the direction," she said. "Not your wish."

On the way back, the wind freshened and the salt edge called its sharp. Taro wanted to mark it. He cut three notches on a stick and set it too close to the shelf where the crust broke. Ya-la moved it a step back. "Tomorrow wants it still standing."

The old woman came as far as the first fork and sat on a rock. "If I get lost, I will blame you all twice," she said, and coughed. "Make your path loud."

They made it loud without shouting. Stones, sticks, braids; shoulder, knee, foot. At dusk, Hakaka said, "Test."

They split. La-la and Taro started from the clay, eyes open. Ya-la and Sa-la started from the river, eyes half-lidded. Hakaka waited at the shelter, listening to the way the ground answered.

La-la called once when she found a place where no mark spoke. Taro jogged back, set a stick, cut one notch clean, turned the flat face until it watched the right way. "Now it talks."

Sa-la missed the far stone at a fork and walked the wrong bend three steps before his shin met the grass braid. He stopped, grinned, and turned. "It works," he said into the dusk, not proud, just sure.

Ya-la paused at the low ground. The braid ticked her fingers. She stepped wide, feeling for firmness with the edge of her foot like a blind thing learning shapes. "Thank you," she told the rope, quiet.

They arrived near dark with only one more pause. Hakaka stood and counted them without moving her head. "Again tomorrow," she said.

"Add a rule," La-la said, rubbing a smear of clay from her wrist. "Sticks face both ways where the path is trick."

"Both ways," Hakaka agreed. She turned to Sa-la. "Say it."

"Both ways where it lies," he said. "So home and away both can hear."

They ate thin stew. After, when the coals talked soft, Taro took three of the shoulder-high sticks from the day’s work and shaved their tops round. He cut a shallow, long groove down each flat, then rubbed ash into it. "When rain comes," he said, "the groove will hold the line a little. Eyes find it faster."

The old woman watched, chin on her knees. "If you fall into a hole after all this, I’ll laugh until I die," she said, not unkind.

Before sleep, Hakaka walked the new path alone once more. She stopped at each mark and laid a finger on it. "Water," to one. "Clay," to two. "No," to the ash cross. She pulled the grass braid tight where it had loosened, then relaxed it a hair so it would give warning without tripping a tired ankle. On the last stick before camp, she cut a tiny notch sideways--just a breath--so anyone could know they were close even with eyes closed. Home lives in the small change.

Near dawn, a hard wind came with a dry face. It shoved the first shoulder-high stick and turned it a finger from true. Sa-la saw it when he woke to piss. He set it straight, then pushed a stone tight against its base. The wind tried again and lost the argument.

When the light was good, they walked the path and counted what the night had moved. One grass braid had sagged; La-la retied it higher. An ash arrow had washed off a stone; Ya-la scratched the "no" deeper with a thorn, then dusted the groove with fresh ash. A knee-high stick had leaned; Taro cut the base sharper and drove it where the dirt gripped better.

"What did we learn?" Hakaka asked.

"Speak twice," La-la said. "Eye and hand."

"Say less, say the same," Ya-la added. "No new shapes unless all agree."

Taro lifted a stone and set it down again with care. "If it can fall, it will. Make it want to stay."

Sa-la touched the grass braid and smiled because he could see it with his fingers. "The ground can talk if we keep teaching."

Hakaka nodded. "And we listen with feet." She looked at the old woman. "Can you walk to the water and back?"

The old woman stood with a dry snort. "I can limp anywhere if you make it worth my bones," she said. She took Sa-la’s arm and let go before he could feel proud. She walked to the first fork, put her palm to the knee-high mark, and tilted her head. "Even a fool could find this," she said, which was how she said good.

They added one last mark at the shelter: three stones in a tight triangle, a small braid across them. Not because they needed it, but because a path should end with a word you don’t need to ask about.

That evening, when the light went soft, they took the long way to the river just to prove they didn’t have to think. The path spoke, and they answered with steps, not talk.

The marks would fade and be made again. The rules would rub smooth and be cut sharp. The ground had learned its first sentence. Tomorrow they would teach it another.

Badge #07 -- First Watch

Mark: loop-snap · ember cap · three-stone bell
Vow: If you hear it, move. If you move, mean it.

They set the line before dusk. La-la hung three pairs of small stones from a cross-stick by the mouth of the shelter. "Wind makes a murmur. Hands make a knock." She tapped each: light, light, loud.

Taro walked the circle, knee high, setting reed loops that would pop if a paw pressed. "Not to catch. To speak."

Ya-la pinched the coal’s clay cap and made sure it turned easy. "If it sulks, wake it. Don’t feed it for pride."

Hakaka drew a line in ash with the point of a rib: six marks, six breaths apart. "Half-night, then trade." She pointed to the marks. "Walk the circle on one, stand on two, listen on three. Again."

Sa-la took first watch. He wanted it. He’d earned it back.

"Don’t be brave," Hakaka said. "Be awake."

The light went. Cold came up from the dirt. The old woman coughed once and settled. The three-stone bell knocked once in the wind, then quit.

Sa-la walked the circle. One. He put a hand on each loop peg as he passed, feeling for slack. Two. He stood, counting his breaths to keep them from getting soft. Three. He listened with his mouth open the way Hakaka had taught him. The night had a small taste: old grass, wet bark.

He checked the coal. It was a red eye under the cap. He turned the cap a finger. The eye breathed better. He turned it back. He didn’t talk to it.

On his fourth circle he almost sat. He didn’t. He put cold ash on his neck and didn’t complain.

Something moved in the scrub. Not wind. The three-stone bell said nothing. A loop popped soft, like a finger out of a small knot.

He didn’t call. He moved.

He dropped by the coal, cracked the cap a hair, and fed it one dry reed twist. Enough to wake smoke, not flame. He took the smolder to the edge and laid it on a scrap of damp bark. The smoke went down and then along the ground like a low thought.

The thing in the scrub sniffed and shifted. He heard pads in leaves, careful and light.

La-la woke without words, already crawling toward the far side with a length of dry thorn branch. Ya-la sat up with both hands on the robe, ready for a pull. Hakaka’s shadow stood where the bell cord tied off. She didn’t speak.

The pads came closer. The smoke reached them first. A quick cough, then a rustle as the body turned. The loop ahead of it clicked and tugged a reed. Sa-la heard the animal stop, think, and choose the other way.

Hakaka lifted the bell cord a finger and let it fall once. The small knock said, Enough. No chase.

The pads went back the way they’d come. A long minute later, the sound was gone. The smoke thinned and lay still. Sa-la set the ember back in its cup and turned the cap true.

La-la felt along the popped loop, tested the peg, and reset it lower. "Here," she whispered. "Step learned." She pushed a thorn into the ground beside it, angled to bite a curious nose. Not to hurt. To warn.

Ya-la touched Sa-la’s shoulder once with two fingers. He nodded without looking.

They didn’t talk about it. They didn’t need to.

Half-night came. Hakaka tapped the ash line--soft, soft, soft, then the end mark. Trade.

Taro slid out from the blankets without grudge. Sa-la made the hand-to-hand: the watch stick, the coal cup, the coil with two new loops. "One popped," Sa-la said. "South edge. I set a thorn."

Taro’s mouth twitched. "Good." He took the stick. "Go sleep."

Sa-la didn’t argue. He lay down and felt the ground as if it had gotten closer. He wasn’t warm, but he wasn’t too cold either. The old woman breathed like a saw that had found the right wood.

Near dawn the wind changed. The bell knocked twice and then was quiet. Taro walked the circle on one, stood on two, listened on three. He added a small stone to the south peg, just enough weight to make the next step speak sooner.

Morning edged in. Hakaka erased the ash line with her palm. "What did we learn?"

"La-la," Ya-la said, "set the thorn where noses live."

"La-la," La-la said, dry, "will set two."

"Taro?" Hakaka asked.

"South edge gets bold when the wind turns," he said. "I made it honest."

Hakaka turned to Sa-la last. "You?"

He looked at the coal cap and then at the loops. "I moved first. Then I thought." He waited to see if that was wrong.

Hakaka’s mouth almost smiled and didn’t. "Enough for a night."

The old woman sat up, blanket around her shoulders. "Someone bring me water before I praise you." Sa-la brought it. She sipped and grunted. "About time."

Before they broke for the day, La-la scratched a small mark under the south peg: a short notch beside a loop. Taro tied a thinner cord there, lower, just so. Ya-la pinched a bit of ash into the cap seam to remind the coal to mind its breath. Sa-la hung the watch stick back on its peg and didn’t touch it again.

The mark would be gone by evening. The edge would not forget. Neither would they.

Badge #06 -- Field Oven

Mark: shallow pit · stone ring · clay lid · draw hole
Vow: Heat likes tight. Wait until the wait turns sweet.

They chose a hard patch out of the wind’s mouth. Taro stomped it flat with his heels. "No roots. Good."

La-la scratched a circle with a stick. "Pit here. Draw hole there." She pointed to a low side.

They dug a shallow bowl with rib and stick. Ya-la picked the sharp stones out and set them in a neat line. Sa-la wanted to toss them in.

"Edges up," La-la said. "Make a floor that doesn’t slide."

They fitted flattish stones into the pit, shimming with gravel until the wobble went away. Taro built a small ring of stones at the lip, two hands high. "So the lid has something to sit on."

Hakaka tested the sit with a flat stone. It rocked once, then settled. "Good."

They needed clay. Sa-la fetched from the river bend in a gourd, gray and slick. Ya-la kneaded ash into it until it held a thumbprint instead of running. "Clay remembers better if it eats ash."

La-la smoothed a lid on a flat board: thick as a thumb, not more. Sa-la pressed his fingers in too deep.

"Don’t make it proud," Hakaka said. "Proud cracks."

The old woman sat where smoke wouldn’t find her first. "If it comes at me anyway, I’ll bite it."

They laid a finger-width tunnel from the pit’s belly to the downslope--draw hole--using small stones and clay to seal a passage. Taro poked it through with a stick until he felt daylight. "Door for breath." He grinned, shy at the joke.

Fire was next. Ya-la built the bed: thin sticks first, then thicker, then a few small knots of dry brush. She struck coal to tinder in the coal cup and fed the baby flame into the pit. The fire took, then sulked, then grew.

"Now we heat the stones until they talk," Hakaka said.

They fed steady, not greedy, letting the heat sink into the floor. Sa-la wanted to heap fuel.

La-la knocked his hand away. "Heat likes tight, not tall."

Smoke drew through the little tunnel like it had been waiting for the idea. The lid sat nearby, drying in the sun, a small crack trying to be born.

Ya-la saw it, wet her fingers, and rubbed clay across until the line blurred. "Live later," she told the crack. It minded her for now.

By midshade the stone floor went dull red in the seams and hummed a little. Hakaka sprinkled a drop of water. It jumped and was gone. "Ready."

They pushed coals to the sides with sticks, leaving a clear belly. Ya-la laid fish split along the back, skin down. La-la slid a flat of dampen root slices in a basket. Taro added a thin loaf--ground seed and water and a pinch of salt--more hope than bread.

"Lid," Hakaka said.

They set the clay lid over the ring. La-la sealed the seam with a rope of clay-ash so mean air had to fight to get in. Ya-la put a small leaf by the draw hole. It quivered with the pull. "Breathing."

Sa-la crouched over the draw hole, eager. Smoke puffed at his face when a gust shifted. He coughed and reached to lift the lid.

Hakaka’s hand closed on his wrist. "Touch it and you start over."

He froze, cheeks hot, eyes watering. He nodded. He sat back on his heels. Ya-la pressed a damp cloth into his palm without looking at him. He wiped his eyes and didn’t argue.

They waited.

The old woman sniffed once, then twice. "Raw," she said at first. After a while she said nothing. Then: "Almost." Her chin lifted, like a dog catching a better wind. "Now."

Hakaka wet a finger and touched the seam; the sizzle was softer. La-la eased the lid a finger’s width, let a pale breath escape, then set it again. "Another bit."

They gave it that bit.

When they lifted for real, heat came up in a gentle wave. The fish had turned firm. The root gave easily to a thumb. The little bread had a skin.

Sa-la reached for the bread. "Mine."

La-la tapped his knuckle. "Ours."

They ate standing. The fish was clean and smoky. The root was sweet. The bread was small and good anyway. The old woman chewed slow and stopped coughing.

Taro laughed once, quiet. "Better than sticks over flame."

"Flame is for hurry," Hakaka said. "Stone is for plan."

Sa-la cut a thin piece of bread and handed it to Ya-la first. She took it and didn’t make it a ceremony. "Thank you."

They set more in, this time thinner, this time with more space so the heat could touch all sides. Sa-la watched the draw leaf for the small changes, made himself count between breaths. He didn’t touch the lid.

A crack finally showed along the lid’s edge when a dry fleck of clay gave up. La-la pinched ash-wet clay over it and smoothed it closed. "Next lid, thicker at the lip."

"Next lid," Hakaka said.

They let the last batch carry itself home in the warm pit while they cleaned tools. Ya-la rinsed the coal cup with a mouthful of water and spit black. "Back to bed," she told the coal, and capped it.

Before dark, La-la scratched a small circle and a short line beside it in the ash corner--oven and breath. She didn’t mind when someone’s heel blurred it. The smell of good smoke had already climbed into their hair and sleeves and wouldn’t forget.

On her blanket, the old woman said to the ceiling, "That’s how bread should taste." No one answered. They all knew.

Sa-la lay awake two breaths longer than he needed to, watching the small leaf at the draw hole go still. He smiled into the dark, not showy. He had waited. He had kept his hands off the lid. It felt like a new thing he could do.

Outside, the oven’s stones let their heat go in small gifts. The ground took them and didn’t complain. They slept. The mark would be gone by morning. The knowing would not.

Badge #05 -- First Weir

Mark: V of stakes · basket gate · green brush
Vow: Let the river work. Leave a way; take the rest.

They walked the shallows where the bank came easy and the water talked without spitting. Taro waded first, heel flat, toe feeling. "Gravel. Holds."

"La-la, stakes," Hakaka said.

La-la had cut them last night: arm-length reeds with their ends chewed to points by fire. She pushed the first into the bed with both hands, leaning her weight until the river stopped arguing. "Angle with the flow," she said. "So it wants what we want."

Sa-la drove the next and set it wrong, straight to the current. The water slapped it and made a silly noise.

"It laughs at you," Ya-la said, mouth not unkind.

Sa-la pulled, reset to match the first. The silly noise went quiet.

They built the arms of a V that pointed downstream. Between stakes, La-la wove green brush and split reeds. "Not tight," Hakaka said. "Let small water through so big water doesn’t take it."

The old woman sat on a rock where she could scold and still keep her feet dry. "If you block a throat, it coughs on you."

"We heard," La-la said, and left two hands’ width between the V’s lips and the river’s middle.

At the mouth of the V they set a basket gate: a coil-work tube Taro had made with boredom and pride, narrow at the tail, wide at the mouth. Ya-la tied it with two loops of line so it could swing and not swim away.

Sa-la wanted one more brush mat to close a gap near the bank.

"Greedy breaks," Hakaka said. She took the mat from his hands and set it higher, where the rush came smoother. "Leave trouble a door so it doesn’t take the wall."

He nodded, chewing the inside of his cheek.

They stood back and watched the water teach their work. Silt curled, then settled. The brush twitched but held. The basket gate nodded in small yeses.

"Now we tire them," Taro said, quiet like a secret.

They did other work while the river did theirs: La-la cut more brush, Ya-la scraped a flat stone clean, Sa-la threw stones at a target and missed, then missed less. The old woman counted clouds she didn’t like.

By midshade the gate shook different.

"Ready," Hakaka said.

Sa-la splashed forward too fast; La-la caught his shoulder. "Walk like a fish that knows it has time."

At the gate they lifted together: Hakaka and La-la on the loops, Sa-la under the mouth. The basket came up heavy with wriggle and shine.

Taro laughed once, quiet and real.

They tipped the catch into a shallow pit lined with wet leaves. Three hand-length fish, two smaller, one that slapped Sa-la’s wrist and made him swear and grin.

"Big ones today. Small ones tomorrow," Ya-la said.

"Not if we eat them now," Sa-la said.

Hakaka flicked water from her fingers. "We eat two. Two we smoke. One goes back."

Sa-la made a face. "Why?"

"Because we want sisters," La-la said. "Let one tell them where the door is."

The old woman coughed her drum-cough. "And because the river likes a thank you."

Sa-la looked at the biggest fish, then at Hakaka. She didn’t blink. He nodded and set the second-biggest back into the gate and lowered it to the seam. The fish hung stunned, then found the slow and was gone.

They reset the basket and left again. The next lift brought mostly small ones. Ya-la let some slide through her fingers back to the V’s inside water. "Too young," she said, and wiped her hands on her thighs.

On the third lift a surge came from upriver without telling them first. The left arm of the V shivered, shouldered, and three stakes tilted toward home.

"Hold!" La-la said.

Taro braced a knee against the strong post and pushed the leaning stake upright with his forearms. Sa-la shoved brush back into the bite. Hakaka put her back to the anchor, feet planted, rope-burn of the river in her hands though she held no rope.

The surge passed like a big thing that had somewhere else to be. The stakes settled. One had split a little at its mouth-bite.

"Next time, thicker at the top," La-la said.

"Next time," Hakaka said.

They tied a diagonal brace across the weak place with spare reed, not pretty but useful. The basket wagged at them like it knew.

By last light they had enough for tonight and a little for tomorrow: four for the smoke, two for the pot. Ya-la rubbed salt and dried green into the skins while the field oven woke and remembered how to breathe. Taro cut slits along the backs so the smoke would touch the meat like it meant it.

Sa-la stood at the river and watched the V work without him. He picked a flat stone and skipped it twice. The third time it sank like a truth.

Ya-la came to stand beside him. "You wanted to close the gap."

"I did."

"You didn’t."

"I didn’t," he said, and sounded surprised at himself.

"Good," she said, and bumped his shoulder with hers.

They eased the basket off and set it on the bank so the river wouldn’t teach it to leave at night. La-la walked the line of stakes one more time, putting her palm on each in turn like counting a breath.

The old woman slid off her rock and felt the ground with her toes. "Weir stands. So will we."

On the walk back, Hakaka said, "Tomorrow we bring longer stakes and swap the split one out."

"Taro will cut," La-la said.

"I’ll cut," Taro said.

Sa-la looked at the dark water. "What if someone takes it while we sleep?"

"Then they needed it," Ya-la said.

"Then we build it better," La-la said.

"Then we learn who they are," Hakaka said, and didn’t make it a threat or a hope.

They ate the two with steam and bread, and hung the four in the oven’s warm mouth to catch night smoke. The house smelled like a promise and like work.

Before sleep, Hakaka took a stick from the ash edge and scratched a small V on the floor where the map lived until morning. She left the mark and didn’t protect it. The old woman’s heel smudged it as she turned on her blanket.

"In us," Hakaka said to nobody, and lay down. The river kept talking. The stakes listened. The gap stayed open. The door remembered.

Badge #04 -- Field Oven

Mark: ring of stones · draw hole · palm bread
Vow: Heat likes tight. Wait until the wait turns sweet.

They chose ground that didn’t drink wind. Hakaka pressed her heel and felt it hold. "Here."

La-la set a circle with thumb-lengths between stones. "Tighter," she said, and pushed each until they found each other.

Taro fetched clay from the bank and sand from the bar. He mixed with dry grass, short-cut, until it held together without shining.

Ya-la found a flat, heavy shard for a floor. She laid it level and checked again when no one was looking.

Sa-la brought coals in a pot with a lid of damp clay. "It’s alive," he said.

"Not yet," Hakaka said. "First the mouth. Then the draw."

They shaped a low dome over a frame of bent reeds. La-la’s hands set the thickness: two fingers at the crown, one at the sides. Sa-la smoothed with wet palm until the surface lost its cracks.

The first mouth they cut too wide. Smoke came forward and sat in their faces.

"Heat runs away," La-la said.

Hakaka pinched the mouth smaller. "Tight."

Ya-la punched a thumb-sized draw hole at the back, low. "So it breathes," she said.

They fed the pot’s coals to a nest of twigs and thin sticks inside. The dome took the fire and darkened. Taro kept the feed small and steady.

The old woman came and stood with her blanket around her shoulders. "If it doesn’t bake, it will still dry my bones."

"Stand out of the mouth," La-la said, and pulled her a half-step aside.

The first burn slumped the dome a little on one side where the reed frame had been cut too thin. Sa-la reached for more clay.

"No," Hakaka said. "Let it set. We learn that spot."

They burned it again, hotter, until the inner wall went from wet dark to dull. Ya-la held a hand at the mouth and counted slow. "Five… six… seven." She pulled back before it hurt. "Good."

"Clean it," Hakaka said.

Taro raked the embers to the side with a green stick. Sa-la swabbed the floor with a damp cloth tied to a branch. The steam carried ash out the mouth.

La-la pinched dough from their last bowl of mashed seed and river meal. She pressed six flats as wide as her palm and not too thin. Ya-la laid leaves on the floor and set the first three.

Smoke tried to thicken again. Hakaka put her finger over the draw. "Hush," she said, then opened it a breath and the air straightened.

They waited with their hands behind their backs.

Sa-la lifted one edge too soon. The underside tore and stuck.

La-la smacked his wrist. "Leave it until it lets go."

He looked at the mouth and kept still.

The smell turned from wet to nutty. The edges lifted by themselves. La-la flipped with a thin stick: one, two, three. The second sides took less time.

Ya-la spread a pinch of salt on the first one and folded it in half. She gave it to the old woman.

The old woman chewed, eyes on nothing. "Bread," she said, as if naming an animal that had come when called.

Taro brushed the floor and set the next three. He watched the dome’s low side. "It browns faster there."

"We use it," Hakaka said. "Edges for edges."

They tried fish after the bread, two small from the weir. Ya-la rubbed them with dried green, laid them on leaves. They cooked quick and clean. The skin blistered and slid off in one piece.

Sa-la smiled with his mouth shut.

La-la pulled a hot stone from the ring and wrapped it in cloth. She shoved it under the old woman’s blanket at her feet. The old woman pretended not to notice and moved her heels closer.

The dome cracked a hair at the crown when a gust pushed down the stack of warm air and the draw pulled hard.

"Next time we knit straw across there," La-la said.

"Next time," Hakaka said.

They fed a little more wood, burned it clean, then let it die. Sa-la wanted to patch the crack then and there. Hakaka shook her head. "Tomorrow, when it’s cold. Clay forgets better then."

They ate on their feet and didn’t talk much. The bread was thin and good. The fish was better because it had waited.

When they were done, Ya-la swept the floor with a handful of grass and left a small pile of pale ash in the back. "For the next start," she said.

Taro set a flat stone as a door against the mouth. It didn’t seal, but it minded the wind.

The old woman touched the dome with the back of her knuckles like it was a forehead. "It will do."

Hakaka counted what they had used and what they would have tomorrow. "We make a paddle," she said. "And a hook to keep fingers."

"La-la will," Ya-la said.

La-la snorted. "I will."

Sa-la pointed at the low side. "I can fix--"

"You can wait," Hakaka said, and put a hand on his shoulder without looking at him.

He waited.

When the light dropped, they checked the draw once more, closed the mouth with the stone, and stood back. The dome held its heat and said nothing.

On the walk home, Ya-la said, "The wait was the sweet part."

La-la wiped her hands on her thighs. "The work was."

Hakaka looked at both and didn’t pick. "Tomorrow we try roots," she said.

Taro nodded. "I’ll bring them thin."

The old woman’s voice came small from her blanket. "About time." Then, softer, "Good."

Badge #03 -- First Weir

Dawn found them at the narrow.

Hakaka stood with her feet in the cold edge and pointed. "Here."

La-la walked the line with a reed and an arm’s length, counting out the width. "Two steps less than yesterday. Tighter."

Taro cut stakes from river willow. He checked the grain, shaved ends clean, stacked them by size.

Ya-la watched the surface. "Fast there. Soft here." She tapped the slower seam with a toe.

Sa-la dragged brush in from the bank, breath loud. "How many?"

"As many as hold," La-la said. "Not more than you can place true."

They set the first row across the shallows. Taro drove the stakes with a flat stone. La-la set the angle with her hands--palm to palm, a shallow bend. Hakaka kept the line straight with a cord drawn low to the water. Ya-la fed brush between the stakes. Sa-la wove it tight.

The current leaned. The mud under the middle slumped. Three stakes tilted and the brush dipped. Water pushed through the soft spot and took a mouthful with it.

"It won’t hold," La-la said.

They pulled the failed run, reset two steps upstream where the bottom felt harder. Taro drove deeper. La-la shortened the spacing. Sa-la packed stones at the feet. Ya-la checked the seam again. "Still fast."

"Make the mouth smaller," Hakaka said. "Let the fence do the pulling."

They built a V, open to the upstream, with a throat at the point. The second try stood until midday. Then the angle proved wrong. Water curled around the near wing and dug a new channel. Two fish slid past the open side like shadows and were gone.

"Wrong angle," La-la said. "It gives them a lane."

Hakaka marked three lines in the wet sand with her heel--was, is, will be--and set her foot on the middle one. "Turn the near wing. Point the throat where the pull already wants."

They shifted the near wing toward midstream by a hand and a half. La-la tested each stake with her weight. Taro cut a tighter throat frame. Ya-la braided a short basket to seat at the point. Sa-la brought heavier brush and worked slower this time.

By late afternoon the fence was set tight. The throat was a narrow mouth with the basket tied in place. Water pressed, hissed through the brush, and went on.

A small fish nosed the wing, ran along, and turned with the fence. It found the mouth and pushed in. The basket shook once.

Sa-la grinned and moved. Hakaka put a hand on his shoulder. "Wait for three."

Another fish came, then another. The basket held.

La-la looked at the span, then at the far edge. "All of it?"

Hakaka shook her head. "Not all."

She put her hand at the far wing and pulled out one tuft of brush, then widened it with two fingers until it matched the width of her palm.

Ya-la nodded. "A small door."

Sa-la frowned. "Why?"

"Because we live here," Hakaka said. "So does the river."

They watched. A fourth fish came fast, found the small door, slipped through, and was gone into the deeper run.

"Good," La-la said. "We keep the fence. We keep the river."

Taro checked the knots at the basket and set a second cord, neat and double.

They took the basket up at dusk. Six fish thumped into the shallow tray: small, silver, enough. Ya-la killed them clean. La-la cleaned them quick. Sa-la gathered dry reed tops. Taro stacked thin wood. Hakaka set a small fire in a hollow where the wind could not steal it.

They ate hot pieces with fingers, quiet and pleased.

Across the span, a fish rose on the far side of the gap and broke the surface once, a clean jump, then sank.

Ya-la smiled without showing teeth. "There."

Sa-la watched the small door and did not try to close it.

Hakaka fed one last sliver to the fire. "Tomorrow we check for scouring. We brace the near wing. We leave the gap."

La-la wiped her hands on wet grass. "It works."

"It works," Hakaka said.

They sat until the light thinned and the fence hummed under steady water, and then they went home.

