Badge #06 -- Oven, Salt & Watch

Mark: ring of stones · vent slit
Vow: Waste nothing that keeps.
	1.	After Door
They checked the weir. The gate held. The basket was kind. Hakaka pointed to the fattest fish and let it go. "Keep the trust," she said. They took the rest without talk.
	2.	Dig
On higher ground they scratched a shallow oval. Taro packed the floor with wet clay. La-la set four stones where pots would sit. "Tight," she said. "Air steals heat."
	3.	Fire Bed
They fed the pit with dry sticks until the stones sweated. Sa-la wanted to cook now. Hakaka shook her head. "Let the stones learn hot first."
	4.	Draw Hole
Smoke puddled. Ya-la cut a thumb-wide tunnel from the pit’s side. She blew once. The smoke slid to the wind. "Draw," she said. The fire sighed in and stood up.
	5.	Salt Edge
By noon the sun burned the flat beyond the reeds. A white crust lay like frost. La-la scraped a pinch, touched it to her tongue. "Good sharp." They filled a small pouch. "For keeping," Hakaka said.
	6.	Cure
They split fish clean. Salt rubbed like grit into silver. Racks stood in a line where wind could pass but dogs could not jump. Taro checked the lashings twice.
	7.	Thorn Skirt
Sa-la wove green thorns into a low fence around the racks. The old woman looked once. "Too pretty?" He pushed one thorn deeper. "Not now."
	8.	Test Heat
A small pot sat on the stones. Water shivered, then danced. La-la dropped a thin slice of root. It softened without breaking. "Oven is ready."
	9.	First Loaves
Ya-la mixed cracked seed with water and a pinch of salt. She pressed rounds on a flat shard. Sa-la tried to hurry the flip and tore one. He fed the torn piece to the fire. "Tax," La-la said. He nodded.
	10.	Taste
They ate a round each, thin and hot. The old woman chewed slow. "Teeth remember," she said. Sa-la smiled with his mouth closed so the hot would not escape.
	11.	Watch List
Evening came. Hakaka set stones in a line: four for night turns. "La-la first. Taro second. Ya-la third. Sa-la last till dawn." No one argued.
	12.	Dogs Again
A smell moved in the dry grass. La-la’s rattle clicked once, then twice. No shouting. Stones lifted. Shapes paused at the thorn and chose easy meat elsewhere. Sa-la let his breath back in.
	13.	First Alone
Near dawn the fire thinned. Sa-la walked the fence. He found a low gap and fixed it with two more thorns. He did not tell himself a story. He just checked again.
	14.	Whistle
He heard a soft note from across the water. He answered with the reed they had been given: one long, one short. A matching answer came back. The cliff-shouldered man did not show. The sound was enough.
	15.	Morning Share
Smoke fish cooled on the rack. Two small strips went into the pouch with a pinch of salt. The river carried it over. It came back with dried tuber slices and three pebbles, set like this: with · soon · again. Ya-la grinned into her hands.
	16.	Rule
They spoke it once: Heat likes tight. Draw likes path. Salt keeps truth. Sa-la said it under his breath until it fit.
	17.	Ledger
In ash, Ya-la marked a ring for oven, a slit for draw, a small star for watch kept clean. Hakaka swept it smooth. "In us," she said. "And underfoot."
	18.	Rest
They lay down where the wind could find them but not steal their heat. The blue at the gate held. The racks held. The night kept its side of the work.

Badge #05 -- Weir, Blue & Story

Mark: forked stake · blue thread
Vow: Leave a door for return.
	1.	Bend
They walked until the river slowed and curved like a sleeping back. Taro tapped the inside of the bend. "Here." Hakaka stood where the pull changed under her heel. La-la knelt and tasted the silt with a fingertip. "Gentle. It will hold."
	2.	Teeth
They cut willow and reed. Stakes went in a slow row--wide open at the mouth, narrow where hands could reach. La-la set the angle. "Lean with the current. Don’t fight it." Sa-la pushed one too straight. The river argued and bent it for him. He nodded, learned.
	3.	Gate
They made a small door from cross-sticks and a loop. Ya-la tied a blue thread at the hinge. "So our eyes remember." The old woman sniffed. "So your feet do too."
	4.	First Sink
They weighted a basket with smooth stones and let it rest behind the gate. Water talked through the gaps. Hakaka listened, then turned one stick a thumb’s width. "Quieter," she said. The gate agreed.
	5.	Watch
Night kept a soft hand on the bend. They took turns. Sa-la counted stars. The old woman dozed and woke and dozed and woke. The robe tugged twice before dawn--enough.
	6.	Lift
Morning was cool. La-la lifted the basket slow. Silver beat at the weave. "Enough," Hakaka said. "Not all." She opened the gate with two fingers and let the fattest fish slide out nose-first. Ya-la watched it vanish and said, "Go tell them we’re not thieves." The old woman grunted. "Yet."
	7.	Law
They ate what they kept. They spoke the rule once, not loud: Take what feeds. Leave what seeds. Sa-la tried the words under his breath until they fit.
	8.	Across
On the far bank, the scar-mouthed woman stood with the boy and the cliff-shouldered man. Ya-la raised both hands. She pointed to the blue thread at their gate and then to the boy’s blue scrap. The woman lifted a fish on a stick, showed its belly, then let it slip back alive. A small nod crossed the water. Two pebbles clinked in their pouch when it came home: with and again. The old woman’s mouth twitched. "Learning."
	9.	Blue
Sa-la crushed berries with ash and spit to mark a reed float. The mess turned a hard sky color on his fingers. "Look," he said, half afraid it would fade. It didn’t. Ya-la tied a thin band of that blue at the weir hinge. La-la wiped her thumb on her own wrist; the stain stayed. "River color," she said. "For river things."
	10.	Small Greed
By noon the basket was kind. Sa-la palmed an extra fish, quick. The robe tugged his shoulder--Ya-la’s tug--without looking at him. He froze, then put the fish back. That was the end of it until evening, when the old woman left a stone where he would stub his toe. He did. He didn’t curse. He took the stone to the water and set it at the gate to weight it better. "Paid," she said, not looking up.
	11.	Clean
Taro raked the silt with a forked branch so the water could breathe under the weave. "Work for tomorrow," he said. La-la showed him the rot starting on one stake. "Cut high. Fresh wood." He cut and set and pushed with his hip until the line sat true.
	12.	Quiet Bridge
They sent a pouch across with a coil of the new blue thread and a small bone awl. It came back with a short reed whistle and three pebbles in a row. Ya-la blew the whistle once. A clear note. The boy across blew back the same. The man did not move, but his stillness felt less hard.
	13.	Dogs
Smell found them before dusk. The robe tugged. Everyone lifted a stone. No panic. First rattle, then a second. Sa-la hummed low instead of shouting. The pack thought twice, then chose elsewhere. Ya-la moved a coal with a stick and didn’t say the thanks he swallowed. The old woman heard it anyway.
	14.	Story Fire
Night brought a small wind. They ate and sat. No speeches. Hakaka tapped the blue knot at her wrist. "Today we made a door and remembered to keep it." La-la added, "And cut what rots." Ya-la said, "And learned a note." Sa-la stared at his toe and then up. "I almost stole," he said. No one flinched. "And I paid," he added. The old woman lifted her cup. "About time."
	15.	Ledger
In ash, Ya-la drew three marks: a gate, a circle for return, a short line for the almost-wrong. Hakaka swept them smooth. "In us," she said. "And underfoot." The river turned in its sleep. The blue at the hinge held fast. The door kept being a door.


Got it. Creating now.

Badge #04 -- Rain, Hurt & Meat

Mark: wet coal · bark strap
Vow: Repair what you break.
	1.	Night of Cold Rain
Wind came hard and mean. Rain hit in sheets. The robe pulled them up before words. Hakaka shoved the coals tighter under clay. La-la dragged the reed wall two hands lower. Ya-la cut a shallow runnel in the floor with a rib so the water would choose a road. Sa-la held the roof line when it wanted to kick. The shelter talked in drips. They answered with small fixes until the dark got tired.
	2.	Morning Count
Everything was heavy. Two seams had popped. The old woman coughed and spat clear. They ate thin and hot. No one wasted voice. Work lined up by itself.
	3.	The Joke That Cut
Sa-la said, "La-la, you sleep loud," and smiled like it was nothing. La-la did not look at him. Silence hardened. Ya-la set a cup down a little too neat. Hakaka said nothing. Sa-la’s smile fell off.
	4.	For What
Later, outside, La-la waited with her hands in the wind. Sa-la came.
"For what?" she said.
"My mouth," he said. "And your work. You held the line. I made it small."
"Good," she said. "Now fix your hands on something useful."
	5.	Apology Strap
By dusk he brought a thin bark strap he had cut and rubbed soft. "For your wrist," he said. She took it, tapped it once like thanks, then tied it herself and pulled until it fit. "Don’t do it again," she said. "The joke, not the strap." He nodded.
	6.	Track
Taro found the slots of a doe where the ground kept cool. Hakaka touched the marks, looked at the wind, and set the way. They loosened the nearest snare a hair. Greed breaks hands. Ya-la checked the knife edge on her thumb. Sa-la tied his sleeves.
	7.	First Hunt
They waited in brush that smelled green and wet. When the doe came, Hakaka rose smooth. One throw. Clean. The world went quiet too fast. Sa-la gagged and turned away, then came back and held the foreleg. Ya-la cried without sound and kept the skin clean. La-la worked like she was counting. Taro breathed slow and steady. The old woman sat on a stone above them and watched, eyes dry.
	8.	Thanks
They set the bones high in a thorn fork where dogs would think twice. Ya-la touched the ribs once. "Thank you," she said. No one argued.
	9.	Meat
The stew was good and heavy. The old woman ate first. "Better," she said. La-la’s hands shook after, not during. Sa-la kept his eyes on the knife while he washed it too long, then stopped.
"We’ll be hungry again," he said.
"Not tonight," Hakaka said.
	10.	After
Rain thinned to a stitch. They sat close. No speeches. Sa-la slid the strap on La-la’s wrist one notch better. She let him. Ya-la tucked a coal in clay for morning. Taro hung the cleaned hide where dawn would find it. The robe rested on the peg and did not tug.
	11.	Ledger
In ash, Ya-la marked one short line for hurt and one small circle for repair. Hakaka looked, then swept both away with the back of her hand. "In us," she said. "And underfoot." They lay down. The roof listened. The night kept its side of the work.
☺️☺️🥲🙂🙂🙂🙂🙂🙂
First Weir

River was low enough to argue with.

"V-shape," Hakaka said. "Let it choose the point."

They stacked stones, wove reed between. Left a mouth.

Sa-la stomped a rock and it rocked back. La-la set it right.

They waited. Water talked. Silver flicked and trapped itself.

Sa-la reached too fast; Ya-la caught his wrist. "Let it tire."

They lifted three fish and one got away on purpose. No one chased it.

Field Oven

They bent green sticks into a dome, smeared clay until it stopped drinking.

"Door small," La-la said. "Heat likes tight."

They burned the frame out. The oven kept its shape, darker, cracked like a smile.

Roots went in on hot stones. Smoke stayed where it should.

Sa-la poked too soon. Hakaka tapped the spoon from his hand. "Wait until the wait turns sweet."

It did. The steam had weight. They ate without talking.

Counting Sticks

"How long for meat?" Sa-la asked.

"Until it forgets to be wet," La-la said.

Hakaka cut a short stick, notched it once. "Day one."

Each morning a new thin cut. Ya-la kissed the last notch with ash. "Done."

They burned the stick and started another. Sa-la kept the black ends in a pouch.

"Why?" La-la asked.

"So I don’t forget how many times we didn’t starve," he said, ears red.

Wind Door

Roof held. Wind still found the seams.

"Door wants a leeward ear," La-la said.

They moved the entrance, hinged a mat on reed rope. It flapped, learned the new talk, quieted.

The old woman nodded like she had told them first. "Now the sky can knock and we can pretend not to hear."

Finger Splint

Taro caught a rabbit and the rabbit bit back.

Finger crooked wrong. He swallowed the shout and went pale.

"Straight, then still," Ya-la said. Two thin sticks, strips of reed, a smear of honey under cloth.

"Why honey?" Sa-la asked.

"So it minds its manners," she said.

Taro breathed slow and did not look at his hand. Later, he used his teeth to tie again and didn’t curse once.

Blue Dye

Berries crushed under Carrier bled on the robe.

"Stain," Sa-la said, guilty.

"Mark," Ya-la said. "Now we see this edge in the dark."

They pressed more berries on the hem, a thin river band.

La-la held up a scrap to the sky. "Blue means river."

"Today," Hakaka said. "Tomorrow it might mean trouble."

"Then we’ll learn that too," Ya-la said.

Pestle

They burned a hollow in a thick stump, smoothed it with hot stones and spit.

Found a round stone that fit a palm. Sa-la called it Beater. The name stayed.

Seeds cracked. Salt softened. The sound was work and comfort.

The old woman slept to it and snored in rhythm. No one minded.

Narrow Crossing

A tree fell and made a bridge that didn’t promise anything.

"Robe off," Hakaka said. "One at a time. Hands low."

La-la went first, face flat. Ya-la next, eyes on bark, not water. Sa-la bounced once; the trunk answered and he stopped bouncing.

On the other bank they all looked back at the narrow. No one said brave. They just tied the robe again and kept moving.



☺️🙂🙂🙂😍😍😍
Draw Hole

Smoke liked their eyes. They dug a pit, stacked stones, laid a clay cap with a thumb hole.

"Draw," La-la said, and lifted the cap a hair with a stick.

Smoke rose where it should. Inside air felt like air again.

Sa-la grinned. "My eyes aren’t soup."

"Keep the hole small," Hakaka said. "Big turns hunger into sky."

First Watch

"Pairs," Hakaka said. "Two hours. No stories."

Sa-la and La-la got the moon’s middle. He fidgeted. She put a small stone on his knee.

"What’s that for?"

"If it falls, you were sleeping."

It didn’t fall. In the morning he showed the round mark on his skin and didn’t say anything.

Story Fire

"Three breaths each," Ya-la said. "No lessons."

La-la told the time the wind stole her hair and a crow brought it back. Sa-la told about the fish that spat at him. Hakaka told the smallest story: "We came here. We stayed."

The old woman coughed and said, "Once I was mean." They laughed and let that be the end.

Spilled Bellies

Taro tripped and a seed pocket split. The floor went small and quick with grain.

Everyone stopped breathing. Then they all moved.

Palms, sleeves, even the old woman’s cup worked like little scoops.

When the last seed hid in Carrier’s shadow, Taro took off his bone hook and set it by the door.

La-la pushed it back to him. "Pay with not tripping tomorrow," she said.

He nodded and tied the pocket higher.

First Frost

Morning bit. Carrier wore a skin of ice.

Sa-la touched it, jerked back, laughed. "Glass water."

"Blanket," Ya-la said, and laid a mat over the bowl at night after that.

They breathed steam and learned to love smoke a little more than they hated it.

Quiet Bridge

Across the river a thin girl showed a braid tied with blue. Ya-la showed her own blue hem and pointed to the water.

The girl nodded once and tied a second braid to the root. When the current pulled, the braids clicked like teeth.

"Talking," Sa-la said, surprised.

"Quiet kind," Hakaka said.

Ash Ledger

They swept the floor, then marked the wall with a coal: a line for rain, a circle for a good day, a quick cross for hurt.

No dates. Just memory shapes.

When the wall filled a span, they wiped it with wet hands and spread ash again.

"Gone?" Sa-la asked.

"In us," Ya-la said. "And underfoot."

Bone Flute

Sa-la drilled a bone with fire and patience until it whistled.

He played soft. The dogs didn’t come. The old woman did and sat near, not looking at him.

"La-la?" he asked.

"Not a door," Hakaka said. "A lamp."

He played at dusk. Work felt lighter by one grain.

Sling

La-la twisted a sling from reed and leather, showed Sa-la the arc that didn’t fight itself.

He missed rocks for a day, then hit one with a sound that made him yelp.

"Don’t practice near Carrier," Ya-la said, already moving it.

They brought down a rabbit. Sa-la went quiet after, hand on its fur.

"Thank you," he said, almost soundless.

No one teased him.

Cold Guest

A man from the far bank stood at the water, empty hands, shaking. The cliff-man watched from above and did nothing.

Hakaka set a basket on the reed line: pebbles, a strip of meat, a blue scrap.

He sent back a fish spine and a flat stone with a hole.

"Net weight," La-la said, testing it. "Good."

They ate separate and went to sleep under the same weather.

First Rule Broken

Sa-la slipped honey with his finger before the share.

The old woman saw and didn’t speak. She just moved the cup where his knee would find it in the dark.

It did. He hissed, then laughed, then in the morning put a full spoon by her blanket without looking at her.

She drank and said, "About time."

He nodded and tried not to smile.

First Roof

They stood under a sky that wouldn’t choose a mood.

"La-la," Hakaka said. "We need shade that stays."

"Stays how?" La-la asked, already measuring with her hands.

"By not arguing with wind," Ya-la said.

They cut reed, bent arcs, crossed them. Sa-la tied fast and sloppy.

"Slow," La-la said.

"Fast gets you food," he muttered.

"Fast gets you fixing," she said, tightening one of his knots until it squeaked.

They laced the arcs with long grass. The frame breathed and did not fall.

Wind came hard once. The roof bent, shivered, held.

La-la looked up. "Fine."

Sa-la grinned. "My knots--"

Hakaka tapped one. It slipped a finger’s width. He shut his mouth and fixed it without being told.

They sat under the new shade. Heat stepped back a pace. No one said thank you. They stayed a little longer than they needed to.

Borrowed Fire

Their coal died again. No one cursed. They just picked up bowls and went to the river.

Across the water, smoke. Sa-la blew the gourd once. Waited. Blew again.

Two puffs rose on the far bank. Then a thin child came to the shallows with a clay pot and a stick of glow inside.

"Trade," Hakaka said, placing a coil of reed rope in the current. The rope bumped away, bumped back with the pot. The glow inside shook but lived.

Sa-la wanted to lift it high. "Care," Ya-la said, her hands already cupped around his.

They nested it in ash like a bird. Breath small. New flame took, not proud, just steady.

"Return the pot," Hakaka said.

Sa-la’s mouth bent. He looked at the clean rim, the smooth thumbprints. He slid it back into the current, slow.

On the far bank the child lifted a hand without looking at them. That was enough.

First Needle

La-la found the bone sliver in a fox’s foot, cleaned it, ground it on stone until it wanted to pierce.

"Thread?" Sa-la asked.

"From reed pith," Ya-la said, twisting, licking, twisting, making a line that obeyed.

La-la tested the needle on the robe seam. It bit; the thread followed.

Sa-la leaned close. "Let me--"

"Hands," La-la said.

He took the robe, pricked his thumb, hissed, didn’t drop it. A small red moon marked the cloth.

"Now it remembers you," Ya-la said, and smiled into her palm.

They stitched the loud seam quiet. When it tugged, it tugged kinder.

Salt Edge

They walked dry flats until the ground cracked like old hands. White crust glittered between the cracks.

Sa-la licked a fingertip, flinched. "Sharp."

"Good sharp," Hakaka said. They scraped a small pouch full with a rib bone.

"Meat will sleep longer with this," Ya-la said.

"Teeth will shout if we forget water," La-la added.

Back at camp they rubbed a thin cut with the white and Sa-la’s mouth made a fish face. The sting climbed his arm, then let go.

"Alive," he said, flexing.

"Good," Hakaka said. "Now wash it."

Dogs at the Rack

They strung strips of meat above a slow smoke. Night brought eyes.

Hakaka stacked thorn branches under the rack. La-la tied a drift of dry seedheads to the legs--the faintest rattle for warning.

"Won’t stop the dogs," Sa-la said.

"Won’t have to," La-la said.

Near midnight the rattle whispered. Hakaka moved first, no rush--just there. Sa-la wanted to shout; Ya-la caught his sleeve.

They clapped sticks, low and mean. A dog leaped for the rack, hit thorn, yelped, ran. One strip fell.

Sa-la reached and almost put his palm in the fire. Ya-la moved the coal with a stick. He swallowed the thanks.

In the morning La-la held up the fallen strip--gnawed, saved, ugly.

"It’s ours," she said.

"No," Hakaka said. "It’s the dog’s lesson. We’ll eat the others."

Line Across Water

They stretched a thin reed line from their bank to a leaning root on the far side. The current shouldered it, pulled, then let it ride.

"Why?" Sa-la asked.

"So words don’t drown on bad days," Hakaka said.

They tied a little basket from grass. Ya-la tucked pebbles in it--one, two, three.

"Three means please," she said. "Today."

"Tomorrow?" Sa-la asked.

"Maybe sorry," La-la said.

They sent the basket. It slid, bobbed, kissed the far root, hung there. A hand from the shadows tugged twice. The basket came back with two pebbles and a dry fish head.

"Answer," Ya-la said, and laughed once, surprised at herself.

Sour Tongue

The old woman drank nettle tea and made a face.

"Bitter as your ideas," she said to no one in particular.

"Drink," Hakaka said.

She did, muttering. After, she set the cup by the door where feet would kick it. Sa-la moved it an inch. She watched him and moved it back an inch and a half.

"Why?" he asked.

"So you learn to look," she said.

He looked. From then on the cup stayed upright. She never thanked him. Her cough eased anyway.

Lost Needle

The bone vanished. No one swore, but the air felt tight.

They swept ash with fingers spread. Sa-la rubbed the floor with his palm and hissed when the needle kissed him again.

"Found," Ya-la said, soft.

He held it up, guilty-proud. La-la took it and put it in a small twist of fiber, tied to the robe at shoulder height.

"So it pokes the right one next time," she said, and Sa-la snorted, half laugh, half agreement.

Flood Night

Rain that had been a rumor became a throat. The river rose while they slept.

The robe tugged them awake before the water did.

"Up," Hakaka said, already lifting Carrier and the seed bellies.

They moved to the ash line on the wall, the mark of last year’s high water--Ya-la’s idea, La-la’s scoff, Hakaka’s quiet acceptance, long ago.

Water came to that line and put its mouth there and no farther. Sa-la put his palm to it. It was a cold animal, breathing.

By morning the bank was smaller. Their marks were not.

First Map

Sa-la scratched with a coal on the swept floor: a curve for river, X for the bee tree, a square for the roof that bent but stayed.

"That’s wrong," La-la said, pointing at the curve.

"It’s how it feels," he said.

Hakaka watched. "Mark the place the bank fell," she said.

He smudged a gap. Ya-la added a tiny circle where the nettle patch grew.

The old woman came, scuffed a line with her toe. "There’s where you always forget to look," she said.

They left the map until noon, stepping around it like a sleeping child. Then they swept it into the ash. Ya-la looked once more before it went.

"It’s still in our feet," Hakaka said.

They nodded, and went to learn the next bend.

