Six ideas → realistic MVP experiment plans (in-simulation only)

1) Quantum-entangled verification → 
classical first
•	Hypothesis: Bell-inspired commitments + succinct ZK proofs deliver 90% of the integrity benefit without hardware quantum.
•	MVP experiment (sim):
o	Build a toy protocol design spec (no code) with: commitment format, randomness beacon, proof size/verify-time targets.
o	Red-team walkthrough of tamper scenarios; whiteboard expected detection paths.
•	Success: spec shows ≤1 KB proof, ≤50 ms verify, binds to run-id + time anchor; attack trees covered with clear mitigations.
•	Stop-loss: verifier cost or complexity balloons; unclear replay protections.
•	Owners: @zk + @sre + @red.
•	Deliverable: 2-page spec + threat-model map.

2) Evolutionary protocol / living genome → 
sandbox with human brakes
•	Hypothesis: controlled, sandboxed "mutations" can improve resilience without risking blast radius.
•	MVP experiment (table-top):
o	Define mutation classes (e.g., threshold tweak, detector weight, guard on/off).
o	Map rollback plan & blast-radius caps (state snapshot, canary gates, manual approval).
•	Success: rollback path unambiguous; 3 "mutations" evaluated with measurable benefit criteria and 100% revertability.
•	Stop-loss: any mutation without deterministic revert path.
•	Owners: @sre + @gov + @red.
•	Deliverable: playbook + promotion checklist.

3) Incentive engine (reputation + stake + privacy marketplace) → 
risk-first
•	Hypothesis: simple incentives may help, but cartel/wealth concentration risks dominate early.
•	MVP experiment (econ sim design):
o	Specify agent types, wealth distributions, and griefing/cartel scenarios.
o	Define fairness metrics (Gini limit, cartel success rate).
•	Success: parameter windows where Gini < 0.6 and cartel success < 15% exist.
•	Stop-loss: no feasible window without heavy centralization--then NO-GO for now.
•	Owners: @mech + @red + @metrics.
•	Deliverable: simulation plan + acceptance band; no on-chain/real staking.

4) Planetary Time (PCT) → 
benefit vs complexity
•	Hypothesis: a byzantine-median of diverse time sources offers neutrality beyond Zurich with clear audit benefits.
•	MVP experiment (design doc):
o	Compare PCT vs Zurich anchor on auditability, complexity, and failure modes.
o	Choose sources (NTP set, blockchain headers, optional GPS) and fault model (f-tolerant median).
•	Success: documented cases where PCT prevents bias or replay Zurich can’t, with simple operator runbook.
•	Stop-loss: added complexity > benefit; defer and keep Zurich anchor.
•	Owners: @sre + @gov.
•	Deliverable: 1-pager with decision matrix & operator checklist.

5) Ambient governance (passive signals, explicit override) → 
consent-first
•	Hypothesis: opt-in + coarse aggregation + periodic DP noise can raise participation without re-ID risk.
•	MVP experiment (privacy modeling):
o	Define signals allowed (e.g., weekly counts; no raw paths).
o	Model attacker linkage risk; set ε bands and k-cohorts for any published aggregation.
•	Success: participation ↑ ≥8% (proxy in survey/sim) with linkage risk ≤5%; opt-out UX copy tested.
•	Stop-loss: any design that needs continuous tracking or identifiable patterns.
•	Owners: @privacy + @ux + @metrics.
•	Deliverable: consent text, data-min plan, DP table, stop-loss.

6) Prediction-integrated policy (markets + decisions) → 
limited scope
•	Hypothesis: forecasts can raise decision quality for a few specific choices (e.g., budget allocation timing), if insulated from manipulation.
•	MVP experiment (policy sketch):
o	Pick one decision type; define forecast inputs (could be survey-based "play money" rather than real markets).
o	Define how forecasts gate or adjust the decision (thresholds, bands).
•	Success: clear, layperson-friendly UX; legal/regulatory low-risk route (no real money).
•	Stop-loss: market manipulation risk or legal ambiguity can’t be mitigated → learn-more or no-go.
•	Owners: @gov + @mech + @ch_compliance + @ux.
•	Deliverable: decision card + UX mock with safeguards.
 
Scorecard (weights & thresholds to use)
•	Default weights: Value 40%, Feasibility 30%, Privacy/Reg 20%, Abuse 10%.
•	"GO" requires: Value ≥4, Feasibility ≥3, Privacy Risk Low/Medium with documented DP bands, clear stop-loss.
•	"NO-GO" if: Feasibility ≤2 or Privacy/Reg = High without credible mitigations.
 
