Short answer: yes--we can (and should) collect Arabic proverbs and align them with equivalents in up to 100 languages, then pick the best line per situation. Below is the tight plan so it works inside the Ledger, the Council, and donor-facing outputs without losing your format.

What the Council/Ledger expects
•	Every proverb = one record with: lang, script, text, translit, gloss, theme, tone, register, source, family_id (cross-language cluster), situations[], culture_notes, ts.
•	Book of 99 ⇒ 999 grows by families (one meaning, many languages), not loose quotes.
•	Kebab discipline for files/IDs: e.g. book-of-99-index.json, family-unity-tie-the-tents.json.

When to translate vs. "borrow"
•	Option A -- Native equivalent (preferred for non-Arabic audiences). Use the target language’s own proverb that carries the same function.
•	Option B -- Arabic + gloss (when Arabic identity is the point). Show Arabic line + transliteration + concise gloss.
•	Option C -- Calque (rare). Only if a faithful literal keeps beauty and clarity.

Decision rule (fast):
1.	Audience primary language known? Use native equivalent (A).
2.	Arabic identity desired (e.g., KALAM brand moment)? Use Arabic tri-line (B).
3.	If no strong equivalent exists, fallback to short gloss with one-line context.

"Meaning-first" fit (how we pick the line)

We wrap every extraction with: pattern → meaning → proverb.

Selector score (0–1):
score = 0.35*theme_match + 0.2*tone_fit + 0.15*register_fit + 0.15*length_fit + 0.1*cultural_fit + 0.05*freshness
•	theme_match: patience, prudence, unity, generosity, repair, resilience, etc.
•	tone_fit: solemn, humorous, urgent, celebratory.
•	register: donor, council, technical, public.
•	length_fit: short for slides/UI; longer okay in essays.
•	cultural_fit: avoid idioms that misfire for that audience.
•	freshness: avoid repeating the same saying too often.

Minimal schema (drop-in)
{
  "id": "prov-ar-tie-the-tents-v1",
  "family_id": "fam-unity-anchor-v1",
  "lang": "ar",
  "script": "Arabic",
  "text": "اربط الخيام إلى عمودٍ واحد، ثم افتح المدينة.",
  "translit": "Irbiṭ al-khiyām ilā ʿamūd wāḥid, thumma iftaḥ al-madīna.",
  "gloss": "Tie the tents to one pole, then open the city.",
  "theme": "unity",
  "tone": "calm",
  "register": "council",
  "situations": ["coordination","merging-streams","pre-launch"],
  "source": "KALAM Book of 99",
  "culture_notes": "Unity before expansion.",
  "ts": "2025-09-08T00:00:00Z"
}
Same family, multi-language examples (compact)
•	ar: «اربط الخيام إلى عمودٍ واحد…» -- unity before scale
•	en: "Tie the tents to one pole, then open the city."
•	de: „Bünde zuerst den Mast, dann öffne das Tor."
•	fr: « Attache d’abord le mât, puis ouvre la ville. »
•	es: "Primero el mástil común, luego abrimos la ciudad."

If the audience is German-speaking donors, we surface de first; we still keep ar in metadata and optionally show it as heritage.

Curation workflow (fits your format)
1.	Ingest: Add your proverbs/anomalies (we keep your 20k/20k as source).
2.	Normalize: ISO-639 lang codes, kebab IDs, dedupe by family_id.
3.	Theme map: Tag each with one of ~12 canonical themes (unity, prudence, patience, repair, generosity, beginnings, endings, resilience, learning, justice, stewardship, time).
4.	Equivalents hunt: For each Arabic line, attach 1–3 high-quality equivalents in priority languages (DE/FR/IT/RM/EN + others you choose).
5.	Quality gates: human check for tone/register; mark cultural redlines (donor/compliance contexts).
6.	Publish: book-of-99-index.json + families/ folder; Council/DIGEST pulls by situation + theme.

Where this shows up
•	DIGEST: one proverb per digest, tri-line if Arabic is central; else native equivalent with Arabic in footnote.
•	Council: opening/closing proverb must match weakest-voice-first annotation (tone: protective).
•	Donor: humor + proverb (Rule-21), never extractive.

Quick starter set (you already have #1)
1.	Unity/coordination -- (family above)
2.	Patience under pressure -- ar: «الصبر مفتاح الفرج.» → en: "Patience unlocks relief." → de: „Geduld öffnet die Tür."
3.	Repair after error -- ar: «الرجوع إلى الحق فضيلة.» → en: "Returning to the right is a virtue."
